Title: Casebook chapter about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

# Section 2(d) of the Lanham Act: Foundations of Likelihood of Confusion

Section 2(d) is the statutory axis around which denial of federal trademark registrations turns when a proposed mark so resembles a previously used or registered mark that confusion among consumers is likely. The administrative process for testing registrability—the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) proceedings—borrows much of its analytic architecture from infringement doctrine, but its purpose, remedies, and evidentiary rules differ in important respects from a civil suit for infringement. The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., No. 2023-2143 (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025), supplies a useful vehicle for exploring how Section 2(d) operates in practice, how the Board applies the DuPont factors, and where administrative and judicial inquiries diverge.

## Text and purpose of 15 U.S.C. § 1052(d)

Section 2(d) provides, in relevant part:
“No trademark by which the goods of the applicant may be distinguished from the goods of others shall be refused registration on account of its nature unless it—(d) Consists of or comprises a mark which so resembles a mark registered in the Patent and Trademark Office, or a mark or trade name previously used in the United States by another and not abandoned, as to be likely, when used on or in connection with the goods of the applicant, to cause confusion, or to cause mistake, or to deceive.” 15 U.S.C. § 1052(d).

The statutory command is straightforward: deny registration where the applicant’s mark and a prior mark are so similar that consumers are likely to be confused as to source. The provision serves a tripartite set of public and private interests. First, it protects consumers by reducing the risk that purchasers will be misled about the source, sponsorship, or affiliation of goods or services. Second, it protects senior users’ ability to exclude confusingly similar junior marks from the federal register, preserving the commercial value of their marks. Third, it preserves the integrity and informational function of the federal register by preventing the issuance of registrations that would be misleading in the marketplace.

Although the statute speaks in terms of marks that “so resemble” prior marks that confusion is likely, courts and the TTAB have constructed a multi-factor test—the DuPont analysis—to translate that general standard into adjudicative practice. The DuPont framework asks decisionmakers to examine the parties’ marks, the relatedness of their goods and services, the strength of the prior mark, the presence of third‑party uses, channels of trade, consumer classes, evidence of actual confusion (if any), and a host of other considerations. Apex Bank illustrates how those discrete factual inquiries cohere into a decision under Section 2(d): the Board found the parties’ services highly similar and sustained the opposition, but the Federal Circuit vacated parts of the Board’s analysis where the Board treated related DuPont inquiries inconsistently.

## Registration versus use‑based infringement: why the standard is similar but the forum and remedies differ

The analysis for refusing a registration under Section 2(d) and for deciding a civil trademark infringement claim in federal court are closely related. Courts and the TTAB rely on largely the same evidentiary considerations—typically the DuPont factors in the TTAB and the Polaroid or similar multi-factor tests in district courts. Both inquiries center on the likelihood that consumers will be confused about the source or sponsorship of goods or services.

Despite the shared doctrinal DNA, important distinctions separate registrability proceedings and infringement litigation. The TTAB’s inquiry is forward-looking and confined to the question whether registration would create a likelihood of confusion; it does not adjudicate rights in the marketplace nor grant remedies for past harm. A TTAB decision can refuse registration or cancel a registration, thereby depriving a party of some procedural advantages that flow from federal registration. But the TTAB cannot award injunctions against use, damages, or an order requiring that an already used mark be abandoned in commerce; those remedies are available only through a district court action.

A district court adjudication of infringement assesses actual use on the market, may award injunctive relief, monetary damages or statutory relief, and can determine priority of use as between actual users. Section 2(d) proceedings, by contrast, resolve the narrower issue whether confusion is likely in the abstract if both marks were used as identified in their applications or registrations. Apex Bank demonstrates this distinction practically: CC Serve, a prior registered user of ASPIRE for credit card services, opposed Apex’s attempt to register ASPIRE BANK for banking and financing services. The Board’s decision controlled only the registrability of Apex’s mark; it did not directly alter the parties’ ability to use their respective marks in commerce or determine remedies that would be available in a civil suit.

## The role of the TTAB in opposition proceedings

The TTAB is an administrative adjudicative body within the United States Patent and Trademark Office charged with deciding inter partes disputes about the registrability of marks. One common device is an opposition: after the PTO examiner approves a mark for publication in the Official Gazette, any party believing it will be damaged may file an opposition within a statutory period. The TTAB hears the parties’ factual and legal arguments, admits evidence into the administrative record, applies the DuPont factors to decide whether confusion is likely, and issues a written decision.

The TTAB’s mission is to police the register. It decides whether a mark should enter the federal register given existing registrations and the facts of record. Because the TTAB’s focus is registrability rather than on‑the‑ground commercial disputes, the evidentiary universe it considers is shaped by the identifications of goods and services in the registrations and applications, and by what the administrative record contains. The Board does not consider the parties’ conduct outside the record and typically does not evaluate use or marketplace facts beyond what the parties introduce administratively. Apex Bank’s procedural posture—an opposition to an intent-to-use application—follows this pattern. The TTAB evaluated the likelihood of confusion under Section 2(d) based on the parties’ identifications of services, the nature of third‑party registrations and uses in the record, and other admissible evidence; the Federal Circuit reviewed that administrative decision for substantial evidence.

The TTAB’s decisions are binding on the registrar and have consequences limited to registration: a sustained opposition prevents an applicant from obtaining federal registration for the challenged mark. Where the TTAB cancels a registration in a cancellation proceeding, it removes the mark from the federal register, but courts, when asked, may reach different results about infringement, priority, and remedies. Parties dissatisfied with the TTAB’s decision can appeal to the Federal Circuit, as Apex Bank did; alternatively, they can initiate a civil suit in federal district court asserting infringement and registrability defenses.

## Burden of proof: petitioner’s burden to show likelihood of confusion; applicant’s rebuttal

In TTAB oppositions under Section 2(d), the opposer (the petitioner) bears the burden of proving that registration should be refused because of likelihood of confusion. The burden is one of persuasion, and the Board assesses whether the opposer has proved likelihood of confusion by a preponderance of the evidence. The opposer typically relies on its priority—earlier use or registration—and marshals evidence under the DuPont factors: the similarity of the marks, relatedness of goods and services, strength of the prior mark, channels of trade, classes of consumers, and any evidence of actual confusion or of consumers’ sophistication.

The applicant (the party seeking registration) may rebut by producing contrary evidence under the same factors. Common defensive showings include evidence that the marks are sufficiently different in sight, sound, and meaning to avoid confusion; evidence that the prior mark is weak or diluted (often through third‑party registrations and uses); evidence that the goods or services travel in distinct channels of trade or target different consumers; declarations, surveys, or expert testimony demonstrating an absence of actual confusion; and proof of concurrent long-standing use without confusion. The applicant can also argue legal points—for example, that the relevant scope of similarity should be narrower or broader for certain factors, or that the opposer has failed to introduce evidence on a material point.

Apex Bank illustrates both sides of this dynamic. CC Serve carried the burden of establishing likelihood of confusion between its ASPIRE registration (priority 1996) and Apex’s intent-to-use ASPIRE BANK filings (2019). The Board accepted CC Serve’s showing on several DuPont factors—most importantly, on relatedness of services—but Apex countered with third‑party evidence of other “Aspire”-formative marks and arguments that CC Serve’s services (as a credit-card program manager) differ from Apex’s banking services. The Board weighed the competing evidence and found for CC Serve; on appeal, the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding on service relatedness but remanded portions of the analysis where the Board had applied inconsistent scopes to related factors.

## The record rule: evidence confined to the administrative record

Administrative adjudication before the TTAB is governed by a strict record rule: appellate courts review only the evidence that was before the TTAB. A party cannot cure a deficiency in the administrative record by introducing new evidence for the first time on appeal. The record rule serves several purposes: it preserves the dominant role of the factfinder (the Board), prevents piecemeal litigation, and ensures that appellate review is based on the evidence the Board actually considered.

Appellate review in TTAB appeals follows the familiar mix: legal questions are reviewed de novo, while factual findings are reviewed for substantial evidence. Substantial evidence means such relevant evidence as a reasonable mind might accept as adequate to support a conclusion. In Apex Bank, the Federal Circuit emphasized this standard when it affirmed the Board’s finding that the parties’ services were highly similar—declining to substitute its own judgment where substantial evidence supported the Board’s factfinding. At the same time, the Federal Circuit vacated and remanded parts of the Board’s analysis where legal error (not a mere factual dispute) had infected the Board’s approach—specifically, the Board’s inconsistent definition of the universe of third‑party marks considered under DuPont factor six. That aspect demonstrates the combination of deference on facts together with correction of legal error under the record rule framework.

Practically, parties before the TTAB must present their full case in the administrative record, with declarations, documentary exhibits, and expert evidence if appropriate, because appellate courts will not normally consider new affidavits or studies. If new evidence is essential, a party may seek to reopen the record in the Board, or litigate the matter in district court where the evidentiary scope differs.

## Comparing marketplace realities with identification‑of‑services control

A recurring tension in Section 2(d) litigation is between two modes of inquiry: the “identification‑of‑goods/services” approach and consideration of marketplace realities. The identification approach—used frequently by the TTAB and sustained by courts—starts from the literal wording in the registrations and applications. Where the identifications are broad, the Board will often treat the questions of relatedness and channels of trade broadly, on the theory that registration gives rights precisely for the identified goods or services, regardless of whether the applicant presently uses the mark in every conceivable subcategory.

By contrast, marketplace realities focus on what the parties actually do: actual consumers, real channels of trade, advertising strategies, and empirical evidence of confusion (or lack of it). District courts, because they adjudicate real‑world disputes and award remedial relief, are often more receptive to evidence of actual marketplace distinctions. The TTAB, however, is not precluded from considering marketplace evidence; it may weigh actual use, targeted consumers, and survey evidence if that evidence is in the record. But the identification of services remains a potent tool in administrative proceedings because it sets presumptive bounds for the services at issue.

Apex Bank highlights the interdependence of these two modes. The Board began its DuPont analysis by determining the meaning of “credit card services” and the scope of Apex’s “banking and financing services.” Guided by dictionary definitions and third‑party registration evidence, the Board concluded that the services overlapped significantly—even treating them as “legally identical, in part.” That identification-oriented finding carried implications for other DuPont factors. When the Board later analyzed third‑party “Aspire” marks under factor six, it unreasonably narrowed the universe of comparable marks to those limited to credit card services alone, excluding marks used in broader banking or financing contexts. The Federal Circuit corrected that error: once the Board has found the services highly similar for purposes of factor two, it must keep that same scope in play when assessing relatedness or weakness under other factors. In other words, identification-of-services control can be decisive, but it must be applied consistently across the analysis and reconciled with any real-world evidence in the record.

For students, the takeaway is twofold. First, the language used to identify goods and services in applications and registrations matters tremendously in Section 2(d) disputes: broad identifications magnify the risk of overlap and a finding of relatedness. Second, administrative factfinders should—and courts will—harmonize identification-based inferences with record evidence about how services are marketed and perceived in the real world. Apex demonstrates a situation in which an identification-based finding (high similarity) required the Board to widen, not narrow, the universe of third-party uses considered under factor six.

## Relationship between Section 2(d) proceedings and civil infringement actions

Section 2(d) proceedings and civil infringement suits are complementary but distinct instruments for resolving trademark conflicts. They overlap doctrinally because both examine factors that bear on likelihood of confusion, and because a TTAB decision may be persuasive in a later district court case. But the two forums offer different remedies and reach distinct types of finality.

A TTAB victory grants registration control: the opposer can prevent the applicant from registering the mark (or obtain cancellation of an existing registration). Registration carries procedural benefits—prima facie evidence of validity, nationwide constructive notice, and a presumption of ownership—but it is not a substitute for actual use rights. Conversely, a district court can enjoin use, award damages, recover profits, and shape equitable remedies tailored to marketplace realities.

The relationship between TTAB and district court adjudications is nuanced. A party who loses at the TTAB may litigate a parallel or subsequent infringement suit in district court; the district court is not mechanically bound by the TTAB’s factual or legal conclusions, though collateral estoppel (issue preclusion) may apply if the TTAB’s determinations meet the usual preclusion criteria (identical issues, actually litigated and decided, with a full and fair opportunity to litigate, and necessary to the judgment). Apex Bank’s decision underscores the appellate path for TTAB litigants: a TTAB ruling is appealable to the Federal Circuit, which reviews legal conclusions de novo and factual findings for substantial evidence. That review can affirm, vacate, or remand. If the TTAB’s findings are vacated in part, as in Apex, the registrability dispute returns to the administrative forum for further proceedings consistent with the appellate court’s guidance.

Practically, strategic considerations shape a party’s choice of forum. An opposer with a strong registration may choose the TTAB route to block a confusing junior registration and preserve its position on the register; a party seeking immediate relief from ongoing infringing use is more likely to file a district court suit seeking an injunction and monetary relief. Parties sometimes pursue both tracks in parallel, mindful of the different burdens, evidence, and outcomes each forum produces.

Conclusion: integrating doctrine and practice

Section 2(d) sits at the crossroad of statutory text, administrative procedure, and common law likelihood-of-confusion doctrine. The DuPont framework furnishes the analytic architecture within which the TTAB and courts weigh competing evidence about marks, goods and services, consumer perception, and market conditions. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is an instructive decision because it shows how a Board’s factfinding about the similarity of services can govern the appropriate universe of evidence when assessing the weakness of a prior mark and the overall commercial impression of the marks. That case also reminds practitioners and students that administrative decisions must apply legal standards consistently across related DuPont factors, that appellate courts will correct legal error while deferring to factual findings supported by substantial evidence, and that registrability decisions, however consequential, are not substitutes for the broader remedies and fact‑intensive inquiries available in civil infringement litigation.

# Appeals from the TTAB and Standards of Review

This chapter explains how decisions of the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB or Board) are reviewed on appeal, what appellate pathways are available to parties who lose at the TTAB, the Federal Circuit’s jurisdiction over TTAB appeals, and the standards the appellate court applies to the Board’s factual findings and legal conclusions. The discussion uses Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., 2025 WL ___ (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025) (No. 2023‑2143), as the primary vehicle to illustrate these principles. Apex Bank is a useful recent example because the Federal Circuit applied the familiar division between de novo review of law and substantial‑evidence review of fact, explained the interplay between factual findings on related DuPont factors, and demonstrated the practical remedies—affirmance in part, vacation in part, and remand—when review reveals mixed errors.

## Appellate pathways: § 1071(a) appeal to the Federal Circuit versus § 1071(b) de novo civil action

When the TTAB issues an adverse final decision in an opposition or cancellation proceeding, the Lanham Act gives the losing party two mutually exclusive options. Under 15 U.S.C. § 1071(a), the party may appeal the Board’s decision to the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit. That appeal is confined to the administrative record developed before the Board. Alternatively, under 15 U.S.C. § 1071(b), the party may instead bring a civil action in a federal district court, where the case is tried de novo—with the ability to introduce new evidence, call live witnesses, and develop a fuller factual record. A plaintiff may not do both; the statute provides an election of remedies.

The practical consequence of this choice is fundamental. An appeal under § 1071(a) is an appellate proceeding: the reviewing court confines itself to the record before the Board and reviews the Board’s findings under the statutory judicial standards of appellate review. By contrast, a § 1071(b) suit in district court is an original trial that substitutes the federal district court’s findings for the Board’s and permits expansion of the evidentiary record. Because appellate reversal of factual findings is difficult under the substantial‑evidence standard (discussed below), parties who believe they need additional evidence to prevail may prefer the de novo district‑court option. But the district‑court path is often more expensive and time‑consuming; parties must weigh the likelihood of success on the existing administrative record versus the cost and delay of a new trial.

Apex Bank illustrates this strategic contrast. Apex chose the § 1071(a) route and appealed to the Federal Circuit. The Federal Circuit therefore reviewed only the TTAB record and remanded to the Board where the court found legal errors in how the Board treated the DuPont factors and the universe of third‑party marks. Had Apex sought a § 1071(b) de novo trial in district court, Apex could have introduced additional evidence about third‑party uses of “Aspire” and the marketplace to support its arguments that the registered mark was weak and entitled to a narrow scope of protection.

## Federal Circuit jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B)

The Federal Circuit’s power to hear appeals from the TTAB is statute‑based. Title 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B) gives the Federal Circuit exclusive appellate jurisdiction over “an appeal from a decision of the United States Patent and Trademark Office” with respect to certain trademark matters. When appellants take the § 1071(a) path, their appeals proceed under that statutory jurisdictional grant to the Federal Circuit. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. invoked § 1295(a)(4)(B) as its jurisdictional basis for review, a routine posture for TTAB appeals.

Because the Federal Circuit has nationwide jurisdiction over these appeals, it provides uniform federal law on trademark questions decided administratively by the PTO. That uniformity also underscores why the precise standards of review and the limits of the record are important: the Federal Circuit applies a developed body of administrative‑law and trademark precedent (for example, DuPont, M2 Software, Coach Services, Jack Wolfskin, Juice Generation) to review Board determinations.

## Standards of review: de novo for law; substantial evidence for fact

A foundational principle in judicial review of agency adjudications governs TTAB appeals: appellate courts review questions of law de novo and review the agency’s findings of fact for substantial evidence. The Federal Circuit stated this division explicitly in Apex Bank, citing M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Commc’ns, Inc., 450 F.3d 1378, 1382 (Fed. Cir. 2006), and other precedents. That division organizes review of a TTAB decision and is particularly salient in trademark oppositions and cancellations where the ultimate issue—likelihood of confusion—is a legal determination informed by underlying factual findings (the DuPont factors).

How does this division operate in practice? The often‑cited articulation in DuPont v. R. P. H. Co., 476 F.2d 1357 (C.C.P.A. 1973), remains the starting point: likelihood of confusion is a question of law based on factual findings relating to the applicable DuPont factors. Thus, when the Board applies legal standards—e.g., how to define the goods or services at issue, which DuPont factors are relevant, or whether a particular evidentiary showing suffices to establish weakness—the Federal Circuit reviews those legal conclusions de novo. When the Board makes factual findings—e.g., the definition of the ordinary purchaser, the nature of the goods or services, whether specific third‑party uses exist, or credibility determinations—the Federal Circuit defers so long as those factual conclusions are supported by substantial evidence in the record.

This structure explains many outcomes on appeal. For example, in Apex Bank the Board’s legal characterization of the parties’ services and the scope of the mark’s protection were reviewed with mixed results: the Board’s factual finding that the parties’ services were highly similar was sustained under the substantial‑evidence standard, and the conclusion that the second DuPont factor weighed heavily in favor of confusion was affirmed. But the Board erred as a matter of law by narrowing the universe of third‑party marks it would consider under the sixth DuPont factor even though it had already found that the services were highly similar; that legal error required vacation and remand.

## Substantial evidence defined and applied

“Substantial evidence” is the statutory administrative standard of factual sufficiency. The Federal Circuit uses the classic definition adopted from the Supreme Court: substantial evidence is “such relevant evidence as a reasonable mind might accept as adequate to support a conclusion” (Consolidated Edison Co. v. NLRB, 305 U.S. 197, 229 (1938)). Applied in the TTAB context, the substantial‑evidence inquiry asks whether the Board’s factual findings have enough evidentiary support in the administrative record that a reasonable factfinder could reach the same conclusion.

Substantial‑evidence review is deferential but not toothless. The court does not reweigh evidence or substitute its judgment for the Board’s on matters of credibility or competing inferences. But the court will reverse when factual findings are unsupported by any reasonable basis in the record or when the Board makes no factual finding on a material issue. The Federal Circuit’s decisions repeatedly emphasize that each DuPont factor presents questions of fact to be tested for substantial evidence when challenged on appeal. See Bose Corp. v. QSC Audio Prods., Inc., 293 F.3d 1367, 1370 (Fed. Cir. 2002).

In Apex Bank the Federal Circuit applied this standard to affirm the Board’s finding on the second DuPont factor. The Board defined “credit card services” and relied on dictionary definitions, evidence of how credit cards overlap with banking and finance, and third‑party registrations to support its conclusion that the services were “highly similar” and partly legally identical. The court concluded that substantial evidence supported that factual finding: a reasonable mind could accept the Board’s evidentiary basis and inferences.

But substantial evidence is also contextual. The court will examine whether the Board’s factfinding was internally consistent and whether legal errors infected the factual analysis. In Apex Bank the Board’s later restriction of the universe of third‑party marks considered under the sixth DuPont factor was inconsistent with its earlier factual determination that the services were highly similar; the Federal Circuit treated that inconsistency as a legal error that required vacatur and remand. In short, substantial‑evidence review requires both the presence of supporting evidence and an adequately reasoned connection between that evidence and the Board’s findings.

## Limits of the record on appeal

A key practical consequence of choosing the Federal Circuit appeal under § 1071(a) is that review is limited to the administrative record before the TTAB. The appellate court will not consider new evidence first introduced on appeal; parties cannot expand the evidentiary universe in a § 1071(a) appeal. The record includes the pleadings, exhibits, testimony (if any), stipulations, and arguments the parties presented to the Board. Motions filed with the Federal Circuit asking the court to consider extra‑record evidence generally fail unless the evidence relates to a purely procedural or clerical issue or the parties first seek to add it to the TTAB record through a stay and remand or a motion to reopen at the Board (and the Board accepts it).

This constraint matters for proof of weak marks (sixth DuPont), fame (fifth DuPont), the nature of the goods (second DuPont), or actual confusion (DuPont’s eighth factor). If a party believes the TTAB record lacks critical third‑party evidence or consumer surveys, the party must either have introduced that evidence before the Board or opt for a § 1071(b) civil action where the district court will hear new evidence de novo. As Apex Bank demonstrates, the Federal Circuit will base its review on the record as it stands. Apex submitted third‑party materials to the Board; the court analyzed those materials. Where weakness or crowded‑field arguments were inadequately supported in the TTAB record, the Federal Circuit’s remedial choice was remand so the Board could reconsider the issue with the proper definitional scope—rather than permitting the appellant to add materials for the first time on appeal.

There is a corollary administrative‑law principle: the party challenging a Board decision must show that the record either lacks substantial evidence for a particular factual conclusion or that the Board made a legal error in applying the law to the facts. Procedural objections to the TTAB’s process (for example, failure to consider a relevant factor or reliance on inadmissible evidence) can also form the basis for reversal when preserved in the record.

## Consequences of legal error versus evidentiary insufficiency: affirm, vacate, remand

The remedies an appellate court fashions after review depend on whether the error identified is legal, factual, or a combination.

When the reviewing court finds legal error, reversal that vacates the Board’s relevant holdings and remands for further proceedings is a common remedy. Legal errors include misinterpretation of governing precedent, incorrect legal standard, or misapplication of law to facts—particularly when the misapplication infected the Board’s factfinding or reasoning. Apex Bank provides a clear example: the Board adopted inconsistent scopes of similarity when it defined the parties’ services for the second DuPont factor but then limited the sixth‑factor analysis to a narrower universe of third‑party marks. Because that restriction was a misapplication of law—there was no principled reason to constrict the sixth factor after finding the services highly similar—the Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor analysis and remanded for reconsideration with the correct legal scope. The court also vacated the first‑factor analysis (similarity of marks) because the first factor depends on commercial impression, and the Board’s reassessment of the sixth factor on remand could alter conclusions about commercial strength and impression. Thus, legal error typically leads to remand for the agency to apply the correct legal standard to the existing record (or to take additional factfinding within the agency’s mandate).

When the reviewing court finds inadequate evidentiary support for the Board’s factual findings—i.e., the Board’s findings are not supported by substantial evidence—the court will set aside those findings and remand for further proceedings. Depending on the circumstances, the court may enter judgment for the appellant if the record compels a contrary factual finding or if the Board’s error was so fundamental that no proper factfinding could cure it without new evidence. More often, however, the court remands so the Board can reassess the facts under the correct legal framework or make additional factual findings. The Apex Bank decision shows both outcomes in combination: the court affirmed aspects of the Board’s factual findings (the similarity of services) because they were supported by substantial evidence, and it vacated and remanded other parts because of legal error in the scope of marks considered under the sixth factor and the ripple effects on the first factor.

Sometimes the appellate court will affirm in part and reverse or vacate in part. This disposition is common in DuPont analysis because multiple independent factors interact to determine the legal conclusion of likelihood of confusion. The Federal Circuit often leaves intact fact findings that are adequately supported while instructing the Board to revisit particular analyses or to articulate its reasoning more clearly. In Apex Bank the court affirmed the Board’s second‑factor finding but vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth and first factors, then remanded the case so the Board could apply the appropriate legal scope to the sixth factor and reexamine the first factor in light of any new conclusion about commercial strength.

For practitioners, this pattern has tactical consequences. If only the Board’s legal reasoning is deficient, the court’s remedy of vacatur and remand gives the Board another chance to apply the correct standard to the same evidence. But if the TTAB’s factfinding lacks support, the appellate court may be constrained: it cannot make new factual findings; it can only vacate and remand (or—rarely—render judgment if the record compels a different factual result). That reality reinforces the earlier practical point: parties dissatisfied with the TTAB should consider whether they need a de novo trial in district court to develop additional factual proof rather than rely on the appellate remedy.

## Practical implications and concluding observations

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. reinforces several practical and doctrinal lessons about TTAB appeals. First, the choice between a § 1071(a) appeal and a § 1071(b) district‑court action is critical. The former binds a party to the TTAB record and the substantial‑evidence standard; the latter permits new evidence but imposes the costs and uncertainties of a new trial. Second, the Federal Circuit’s division of labor—de novo review of legal conclusions and substantial‑evidence review of facts—governs outcomes and shapes litigation strategy. When the Board’s legal analysis is faulty, appellate courts will not hesitate to vacate and remand. When the Board’s factual findings hang on sufficient evidentiary support, they will be sustained even if another reasonable factfinder might have drawn different inferences. Third, the interdependence of DuPont factors means that an error on one factor can infect others; appellate courts will often remand for the Board to reconsider related factors together in light of corrected legal standards.

For law students and practitioners, the Apex Bank decision is an instructive model for how the Federal Circuit applies these principles. Read the opinion to see the familiar patterns: the court’s careful parsing of what is law and what is fact, its deference to well‑supported Board findings, and its willingness to correct legal missteps and return the matter to the agency for further proceedings. Understanding these appellate mechanics is essential to advising clients about forum selection, building an administrative record, and framing issues for appeal.

# The DuPont Framework: A Flexible, Factor-Based Test

The Supreme Court of the Court of Customs and Patent Appeals framed the now-familiar multi-factor inquiry that the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) and the courts apply to disputes over likelihood of confusion. That decision, commonly referred to as DuPont, did not create a checklist to be applied mechanically; rather it supplied a set of factual considerations that permit a holistic, context-sensitive judgment about whether consumers are likely to be confused as to source. The Board and the courts restate this principle constantly: likelihood of confusion is a legal conclusion drawn from underlying factual findings—findings that must be supported by substantial evidence on appeal. The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., decided in 2025, illustrates many of the practical and doctrinal contours of the DuPont framework: it confirms that not all factors are relevant in every case, that factual findings in one factor typically inform other factors, and that an inconsistent or artificially narrow treatment of related factors can require remand.

This chapter surveys the DuPont factors, explains their purposes, discusses how tribunals weigh and interrelate the factors, and gives practical guidance for litigating and deciding trademark opposition and cancellation proceedings, with particular attention to financial-services disputes. Apex Bank serves throughout as an illustrative vehicle because the opinion grapples with relatedness, third-party evidence of similar marks, commercial strength, and the proper scope of analysis when services overlap.

## Overview of the DuPont factors and their purposes

DuPont enumerated a suite of considerations—thirteen in the original articulation—that guide the likelihood-of-confusion inquiry. The list is descriptive rather than exhaustive; the Board need not mention every factor, and some will be irrelevant in a given dispute. The factors are best understood as different lenses through which to view the core question: would an ordinary consumer viewing these marks and the goods or services involved be likely to conclude they come from the same source?

A concise articulation of the DuPont factors includes: (1) the similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression; (2) the relatedness of the goods or services and the circumstances of their marketing; (3) the established, likely-to-continue nature of any trade channels; (4) the degree and conditions of buyer care; (5) the fame or strength of the prior mark; (6) the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods (third-party use); (7) the nature and extent of any actual confusion; (8) the length and exclusivity of concurrent use without evidence of confusion; (9) the variety of goods on which the mark is used (i.e., breadth of product line); (10) the market interface—whether the mark is used on goods that a single entity would commonly produce or provide; (11) the extent of potential bridging of product lines; (12) the defendant’s intent in adopting the mark; and (13) any other established probative facts (a catchall).

Each factor serves a purpose. The first factor probes whether the marks themselves create the risk of misattribution of source. The second factor, perhaps the most frequently dispositive, measures the marketplace proximity of the parties’ offerings—identical goods or services or those marketed through the same channels increase confusion risk. The fifth and sixth factors explore source-identifying strength: a famous or conceptually strong mark enjoys broader protection, whereas a crowded field of similar marks suggests that consumers are accustomed to distinguishing among similar marks and that a mark is relatively weak. Actual confusion, when proved, offers strong contemporaneous evidence that consumers are confused in the marketplace. Intent and the conditions of purchasing (impulse vs. careful decision) likewise inform the likelihood that similarity will translate into confusion. Viewed together, these factors help a tribunal shape a calibrated conclusion about confusion risk.

It is critical to remember that DuPont is flexible: courts and the TTAB have consistently said that not every factor is relevant in every case, and a tribunal need only address those with evidentiary support. In the Apex Bank case, the Board focused on the relatedness of services (factor two), the number and nature of similar marks (factor six), and the similarity of the marks (factor one), among other considerations; the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s assessment of relatedness but remanded for a coherent treatment of factor six and, because of interdependence, factor one.

## Weighting and interdependence among factors

A central feature of the DuPont framework is that factors are neither numerically nor hierarchically fixed. No factor is inherently dispositive in every case. Instead, the weight assigned to any factor depends on the case’s context and the evidentiary record. Courts avoid a mechanical “point count” in favor of a qualitative balancing. The Federal Circuit has repeatedly emphasized that some factors commonly carry more weight—relatedness of goods and similarities in channels of trade and consumers often prove determinative—but the tribunal’s role is to evaluate how the particular mix of evidence maps onto the statutory concern: source confusion.

Interdependence among factors is both inevitable and important. A factual finding under one factor will often shape the analytic frame for another. For example, a determination that the parties’ services are identical or highly similar (factor two) should inform the scope of the relevant consumer class, channels of trade, and even the universe of third‑party marks that are pertinent under factor six. Apex Bank v. CC Serve exemplifies this principle. There, the Board concluded in its second-factor analysis that the parties’ services—credit card services and banking and financing services—were highly similar and in part legally identical. Once that finding was made, the universe of “similar goods and services” for purposes of evaluating third-party marks (factor six) necessarily expanded to include related banking and financing services, not only marks connected specifically to credit card services. The Board’s later decision to limit factor-six consideration to marks for credit card services thereby created an analytical inconsistency; the Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s factor-six finding and remanded.

This interdependence means that a tribunal’s sequencing and framing of findings matters. Early, clear findings about the scope of goods or services and about the relevant consumer group should shape the other inquiries. Further, the conceptual strength or commercial fame found under factor five will influence how the tribunal treats third‑party uses under factor six and how it interprets the marks’ overall commercial impressions under factor one. Likewise, a showing of actual confusion may dwarf weaker evidence elsewhere. Judges and practitioners must therefore resist the temptation to treat factors in isolation; instead the tribunal should articulate how specific factual findings interact and how those interactions inform the ultimate legal conclusion.

## What qualifies as a relevant factor on the record

DuPont explicitly allows the Board to consider only the factors that are both relevant and supported by the record. Relevance, in turn, depends on the nature of the parties’ marks and the available evidence. A factor that turns on an evidentiary showing—such as actual confusion, length of concurrent use, or consumer sophistication—will be irrelevant unless the party offers admissible evidence on the point. This limitation preserves the evidentiary underpinnings of administrative decision-making and avoids speculative findings.

Practical sources of admissible evidence include: the pleaded registrations and their identifications of goods or services; third‑party registrations; declarations or testimony about marketplace practices; third‑party advertisements and websites showing use in commerce; consumer surveys; evidence of actual confusion (emails, declarations, mistaken transactions, social-media posts); documents showing marketing and distribution channels; and proof of fame (sales figures, advertising expenditures, press coverage). The board may also draw rational inferences from the record—for example, treating statutory identifications as sufficient to deem goods identical—but courts will set aside inferences not supported by substantial evidence.

The Apex Bank opinion teaches a clear lesson: the Board must be careful that its evidentiary boundaries are consistent. After finding high similarity between services, it was not permissible to limit factor-six consideration to a narrower slate of third‑party marks without explaining why those other marks were irrelevant despite their links to banking and financing services. If an applicant wants the Board to treat a factor as irrelevant, it must show that no admissible evidence bears on that factor. Conversely, opponents should marshal evidence on factors likely to be dispositive—relatedness, channels of trade, third‑party uses, and consumer sophistication are often fruitful hunting grounds.

## Commonly dispositive factors in TTAB practice

Although DuPont rejects categorical rankings, TTAB practice exhibits recurring patterns. The two most frequently decisive factors are the similarity of the marks (factor one) and the relatedness of the goods or services (factor two). If the marks are nearly identical and the goods or services are the same or closely related, likelihood of confusion often follows. Other factors that frequently tip the balance include the channels of trade and classes of consumers (under factors two and four), the strength or fame of the prior mark (factor five), and evidence of actual confusion (factor seven).

Strength (factor five) often acts as a force multiplier: a strong, distinctive mark commands a broad scope of protection. Conversely, a registrant that can show numerous third‑party uses of similar marks in the same commercial space (factor six) can persuade the Board to narrow protection. Evidence of a crowded field tends to cut against a finding that an applicant’s mark should be barred. Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin, and Palm Bay exemplify cases in which third‑party use and registrations influenced the scope of protection. In Apex Bank, the Board and the Federal Circuit turned squarely to factor six because a number of third‑party “Aspire” marks existed in the financial-services universe; the disagreement centered on whether the Board had properly defined the relevant universe of similar marks.

Actual confusion, while powerful, is often difficult to prove because the period between adoption and confusion tends to be short in many cases. Still, where evidence of actual confusion is reliable and contemporaneous, it can be decisive because it demonstrates what consumers are actually doing, not what legal inferences predict. Purchaser sophistication and purchasing conditions also frequently matter: expensive, considered purchases reduce the likelihood of confusion, whereas inexpensive or impulse purchases increase it. In the financial-services context, courts examine the degree of consumer care with attention to whether customers make deliberate choices (opening accounts, choosing financial-service providers) or rely on brand shortcuts.

## How courts articulate and balance factors in financial services disputes

Financial services present distinctive analytical challenges. Banks, credit-card processors, program managers, and other financial-service providers routinely collaborate, license, or co-brand. Those partnerships mean that services can be marketed under the name of one company yet managed by another, and that any one financial institution may offer a wide variety of services under different marks. This marketplace reality often blurs the line between bona fide competition and co-promotion or co-branding, making the relatedness inquiry (factor two) particularly salient.

In Apex Bank, CC Serve owned a federal registration for ASPIRE for “credit card services” and in practice partnered with banks to issue ASPIRE-branded cards; Apex Bank sought to register ASPIRE BANK for banking services. The Board—correctly, in the Federal Circuit’s view—recognized that customary dictionary definitions and third‑party registrations could show that “banking” and “credit card services” overlap. Even when a party operates as a program manager (a back‑office provider) rather than a depository bank, its services may still be related to “banking and financing services” as a practical matter because issuing and servicing credit cards is one way of extending consumer credit. Thus, identified services and real-world relationships between entities are both relevant.

Courts in financial services disputes also pay close attention to third‑party registrations and uses because the market is often crowded with similar marks. If many entities in the financial-services space use the same or similar formative terms—Aspire, Premier, Trust, etc.—that evidence can show the public distinguishes among such marks and that a given word may be conceptually weak in that sector. In turn, a finding that a mark is commercially weak will narrow its scope of protection and lessen confusion risk. Apex Bank underscores that when the Board finds the parties’ services are highly similar, the Board must treat third‑party marks across the related spectrum as relevant for factor-six analysis; narrowing the consideration to a subset (e.g., only credit-card-marketing marks) is inconsistent if the broader services are deemed similar.

Finally, financial services often involve sophisticated purchasers—business customers choosing payment processors or consumers opening bank accounts after researching rates and fees. But sophistication is not automatic; the Board must consider exactly which consumers are likely to encounter the marks. Apex demonstrated that the Board must coherently define the relevant consumer, typically shaped by the services’ identifications, marketing channels, and prices. If credit-card products are marketed broadly to average consumers, the Board cannot assume a high degree of care simply because “banking” can involve complex products. Thus, careful factual findings about purchaser sophistication are essential.

## Practical sequencing of factor analysis

Because of interdependence among DuPont factors, an effective structure for analysis benefits both decisionmakers and advocates. A recommended, practice‑oriented sequence proceeds from the most framing issues to the more derivative ones:

Begin by defining the marks themselves and the identifications of goods or services in the registrations or applications. This exercise frames the rest of the inquiry. The Board frequently relies on the statutory identification to determine goods/services identity or relatedness; where the identification is broad, the Board will treat the services as encompassing all ordinary channels of trade unless the record shows otherwise.

Next, resolve relatedness of goods or services (factor two) and define the relevant consumer and channels of trade. These findings are foundational: they set the scale for what “similar” means elsewhere. If the services are identical or overlap, then third‑party marks used on any of those services become relevant for factor six; if services are unrelated, the universe of third‑party evidence is narrower.

With knowledge of the relevant consumer and trade channels, assess the similarity of the marks in their entirety (factor one). Consider appearance, sound, connotation and the commercial impression the marks create for the defined consumer. Because commercial impression is shaped by how consumers encounter the marks, this analysis should incorporate the relatedness and trade-channel determinations.

Having framed the marks, evaluate mark strength and third‑party use (factors five and six). Determine whether the prior mark is conceptually or commercially strong; analyze the quantity and nature of third‑party uses in the identified goods or services. Make sure the scope of third‑party considerations aligns with the services the tribunal has already found similar—Apex Bank demonstrates the error of narrowing the factor-six universe after determining service overlap.

Then examine consumer care (factor four) and any evidence of actual confusion, length of use, and defendants’ intent (factors seven, eight, and twelve). These factors often fine-tune the result; a showing of actual confusion is weighty, but lack of confusion during a lengthy period of concurrent use can cut the other way.

Finally, synthesize: explain how the factual findings cohere and why they lead to a legal conclusion about likelihood of confusion. Avoid rote counting of factors; instead articulate which factual findings are decisive and how lesser findings support the ultimate legal judgment.

A concrete illustration: in a remand following Apex Bank, the Board must first explain precisely why “banking and financing services” and “credit card services” are highly similar—e.g., by citing dictionary definitions, the parties’ own practices, and third‑party registrations. It must then identify the relevant consumer and trade channels (are these services marketed to retail consumers, to businesses, or both?). With those boundaries set, the Board should reexamine third‑party uses of “Aspire” across banking and financial-service offerings broadly, not only those limited to credit cards. That broader review will inform whether ASPIRE is commercially weak and thus entitled only to a narrow scope of protection; that re-assessment in turn affects the marks’ commercial impressions under factor one. The Board should articulate these connections in a manner that allows meaningful appellate review and that respects the Federal Circuit’s instruction to maintain consistent scope across factors.

## Closing guidance for litigators and adjudicators

Two practical points follow from the DuPont regime and from Apex Bank. First, litigants must shape their evidentiary strategy to the likely dispositive factors. When services are similar, the battle often revolves around strength and third‑party use; parties should therefore marshal persuasive market evidence—third‑party use exhibits, competitor registrations, advertising spend, and consumer surveys. When goods are dissimilar, focus on channels of trade, purchaser sophistication, and the marks’ visual and aural differences. Evidence of actual confusion is valuable but needs to be carefully authenticated and contemporaneous to be persuasive.

Second, adjudicators should structure their opinions to reveal the factual scaffolding that supports legal conclusions. Because DuPont invites context-dependent balancing, decisionmakers must explain not only what they concluded on each factor but why the findings cohere. Apex Bank teaches that when a tribunal finds strong similarity between goods or services, it must not retreat into a narrower conception when reviewing other factors. Coherence and transparency in sequencing, scope, and evidentiary reasoning are essential to both sound decisionmaking and intelligible appellate review.

The DuPont framework is not formulaic. It is a tool for translating factual patterns into reasoned legal conclusions about consumer perception. Apex Bank highlights the flexibility and the constraints of that tool: factors can and should be weighted according to context, but factual findings must be internally consistent across the analysis. Mastery of DuPont requires both careful evidentiary preparation and disciplined, holistic reasoning.

# Relatedness of Goods and Services (DuPont Factor 2): Broad Identifications and ‘Legal Identity in Part’

One of the most frequently dispositive inquiries in a Section 2(d) likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis is whether the applicant’s goods or services are the same as, or related to, the registrant’s goods or services. That inquiry is embodied in the second DuPont factor: “the similarity of the goods or services as described in an application or registration.” DuPont, 476 F.2d at 1361. The Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) and the Federal Circuit approach this question with a set of working rules that emphasize the text of the identifications in the pending application and the registration(s) at issue, not the idiosyncrasies of a party’s actual current business. The governing idea is practical: the public evaluates source using the goods and services listed on the face of the application or registration, and registration confers rights based on what is claimed, not on what is currently sold. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. provides a useful, recent illustration of these principles in action.

This chapter explains the doctrine that governs DuPont factor two, emphasizing (1) why identifications—rather than actual current use—control in TTAB cases; (2) how broadly worded identifications are interpreted; (3) the presumptions about channels of trade and classes of consumers that follow from unrestricted identifications; (4) the use of dictionary and industry evidence to construe service descriptions; (5) the probative role of third‑party registrations; (6) what courts mean by services being “legally identical, in part,” and how that conclusion affects the weight given to factor two; and (7) the particular application of these points in Apex Bank v. CC Serve.

## Why identifications, not actual current use, control in TTAB cases

In opposition and cancellation proceedings before the TTAB, the primary evidentiary focus is the identifications in the filed application and in the cited registration. The Board “looks to the goods and/or services as they are identified in the application and registration,” and applies a legal, not strictly factual, inquiry into whether those identifications describe the same or related goods/services. See M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Commc’ns, Inc., 450 F.3d 1378, 1381–82 (Fed. Cir. 2006) (explaining that likelihood of confusion is a legal determination grounded in factual findings under the DuPont factors). The rule rests on two pragmatic premises. First, an applicant or registrant may claim rights in any goods or services encompassed by the words of the identification, so the Board must treat the listing as its outer bounds. Second, the scope of consumer perception and marketplace overlap is often determined by descriptive language rather than by a registrant’s particular sales or marketing history; registration gives constructive notice of the goods or services described.

Because of these premises, the Board routinely compares the plain language of the identifications. If the enrolled language is broad enough to encompass the same goods or services as the cited registration, the Board can find those goods/services “legally identical” even where the parties’ actual operations do not overlap. That legal identity often proves decisive: when identifications are identical, the relatedness inquiry is resolved in the registrant’s favor without need for extrinsic evidence that the goods/services are used together or marketed through the same channels. Courts and the TTAB have repeatedly upheld this rule: identical or encompassing identifications establish identity as a matter of law, and do so whether or not the parties themselves are contemporaneously competing in the marketplace.

Nonetheless, the Board is not blind to actual use. If a party submits evidence showing that the goods or services, as actually provided, are markedly different or occupy distinct channels/consumers, that evidence can rebut the presumption that broad identifications create overlap. But such evidence must be in the record; absent it, identifications control.

## Interpreting broadly worded identifications (e.g., “banking and financing services”)

Broad identifications are common in trademark applications. Words such as “banking,” “financial services,” “software,” or “consulting” may encompass wide swaths of commercial activity. Interpreting these phrases begins with their ordinary and industry meanings; the TTAB and the Federal Circuit construe them in light of dictionary definitions, industry practice, and the built‑in legal principle that a party owns rights in the full scope of the written identification.

A broad phrase can sweep in services that might, in a narrower factual sense, appear distinct. For example, “banking and financing services” can, depending on ordinary usage, include credit card issuance and account management, consumer loans, deposit accounts, payment processing, and similar services that fall within the ordinary ambit of a bank’s activity. If a registration identifies “banking and financing services” and an applicant identifies “credit card services,” the identifications may overlap because credit card services can be a species of financial services. The overlap need not be complete; it suffices that the identifications encompass some identical or closely related services.

Interpreting the phrase requires ordinary‑meaning tools. Courts often turn to dictionaries to define contested words; they also consider industry evidence—trade publications, websites, or statutory/regulatory definitions—when those materials provide specialized meanings. The central question is what an ordinary consumer or buyer (or, where relevant, a person in the trade) would understand the terms to include. This interpretive step is crucial because subsequent DuPont factors—channels of trade, classes of consumers, third‑party usage—depend on the scope established here.

## Channels‑of‑trade and classes‑of‑consumers presumptions for unrestricted identifications

When identifications are unrestricted—that is, they include no limitations as to trade channels, purchasers, or use—the Board presumes that the goods or services travel in all normal channels of trade for those services and will be purchased by all normal classes of consumers for those services. This presumption is powerful. It follows directly from the TTAB’s primary reliance on the identifications: if an identification covers all banking services, then the Board will assume the applicant’s and registrant’s services could be offered through the same channels and to the same consumers, absent evidence to the contrary.

The practical consequence: an applicant cannot defeat a finding of relatedness by asserting, without proof, that its services are offered only online or only to a niche class of sophisticated commercial buyers. To overcome the statutory presumption, the applicant must introduce evidence that the actual channels of trade or consumer classes are distinct—for example, contracts showing a registrant sells only to large institutions, customer lists, targeted advertising demonstrating nonoverlap, or testimony about product distribution. In TTAB practice, a litigant who wishes to avoid the presumption should narrow the identification at the application stage or be prepared to produce evidence that shows the intended narrower scope of use.

## Use of dictionary definitions and industry evidence to construe service descriptions

Because identifications are textual, the TTAB and reviewing courts often resort to extrinsic sources to construe them. Dictionaries supply readily accessible definitions for ordinary words (e.g., “banking,” “financial”), while industry sources illuminate specialized uses. The Apex Bank v. CC Serve opinion exemplifies this approach: the Board found that the phrase “credit card services” included activities such as issuing credit cards, processing payments, managing accounts, and providing credit monitoring and counseling. When the ID Manual entry for “credit card services” had been deleted, the Board relied on dictionary definitions and other evidence to determine what the service term encompassed.

In practice, litigants should submit dictionaries, statutory definitions, regulatory materials, trade glossaries, and industry websites to anchor the plain‑meaning interpretation. Courts will credit authoritative industry materials showing common usage; conversely, where a term has a specialized meaning in a trade, the Board will treat that usage as probative. The parties’ own admissions and testimony about the services at issue are also relevant. Ultimately, the construal should be anchored in how a reasonable consumer or buyer would understand the language of the identification.

## Third‑party registrations as evidence that goods or services may emanate from a single source

Third‑party registrations and evidence of third‑party use play two related roles in the DuPont analysis. First, they supply evidence that different entities have used similar marks on the same or similar goods/services—showing that multiple producers can provide those services under similar branding. The Board treats this as evidence that consumers are accustomed to different sources offering similar services under similar marks, which tends to show the cited mark is relatively weak and entitled to a narrower scope of protection. See Palm Bay Imps., Inc. v. Veuve Clicquot Ponsardin Maison Fondee En 1772, 396 F.3d 1369, 1373 (Fed. Cir. 2005).

Second, third‑party registrations support the relatedness analysis by demonstrating marketplace overlap: if many firms have registered the same mark for both credit card services and other banking services, that suggests the services are often provided together by a single source. The Board in Apex considered third‑party registrations covering both “credit card” and broader “banking and financing” services to support the conclusion that the two types of services may emanate from a single source.

Caveats are important. Third‑party registrations are not conclusive proof; they are evidence of market possibilities, not definitive proof of consumer perception. Courts look at both the number and quality of third‑party uses. A handful of isolated registrations may not be persuasive; a “considerable number” or “voluminous” showing—coupled with evidence of actual marketplace use—carries more weight. Cases like Juice Generation, Inc. v. GS Enters., LLC, 794 F.3d 1334 (Fed. Cir. 2015), and Jack Wolfskin Ausrustung Fur Draussen GmbH & Co. KGAA v. New Millennium Sports, S.L.U., 797 F.3d 1363 (Fed. Cir. 2015), illustrate how courts consider the aggregate force of such evidence in assessing a mark’s relative weakness and the likelihood of confusion.

## The concept of “legally identical, in part” and its effect on weighting Factor 2

The phrases “identical,” “legally identical,” and related formulations have precise practical meaning in TTAB and Federal Circuit jurisprudence. Goods or services are “identical” when the identifications in the application and the registration literally cover the same terms. They are “legally identical” when, although not phrased identically, one identification encompasses the other; for example, “banking and financing services” may encompass “credit card services,” making some of the services legally identical even if other parts of the identification are not.

When the Board finds that services are legally identical, or legally identical in part, that finding tends to carry substantial weight in favor of confusion. The reason is straightforward: identical identifications eliminate the need for extensive proof of relatedness—consumers who encounter both marks associated with the same type of services can reasonably be confused about source. The Apex opinion used the language “legally identical, in part” to describe the relationship between CC Serve’s “credit card services” registration and Apex’s “banking and financing services” identification: issuing and managing credit cards fall within the ordinary scope of banking and financing.

But “legally identical in part” does not always dictate the outcome. The Board must still consider other DuPont factors—such as the similarity of the marks (factor one), the channels of trade and classes of consumers (factor four), fame or strength of the mark (factor three), and the presence of numerous similar marks in the market (factor six). An applicant can, by competent evidence, counter the implications of legal identity—for example, showing that in practice the registrant’s “credit card services” are provided only through co‑branding relationships with large institutions, or that the services target a different class of consumers—thus removing or attenuating the realistic risk of confusion.

In short: a finding that services are “legally identical, in part” gives factor two substantial force, but it is one piece of the multi‑factor DuPont mosaic. The Board must maintain internal consistency across factors: if it determines the services are broad and overlapping for factor two, it should use the same scope when evaluating third‑party evidence and the commercial strength of the marks in factor six.

## Application in Apex Bank v. CC Serve: credit card services within banking and financing

Apex Bank v. CC Serve illustrates the operation and limits of these rules. CC Serve owned a 1998 registration for ASPIRE in connection with “credit card services.” Apex filed intent‑to‑use applications to register ASPIRE BANK for “banking and financing services.” The TTAB concluded, and the Federal Circuit affirmed as to factor two, that the parties’ services were highly similar. How did the Board and court reach that result?

First, the Board construed the identifications. It determined—using dictionary definitions and an examination of the plain meaning of the terms—that “credit card services” reasonably encompassed issuing and managing credit cards, processing payments, account management, credit authorization, and related services. It also determined that “banking” and “finance” ordinarily include extending credit and providing funds, which implicates credit card issuance and account servicing. Because the wording of Apex’s identification could be read to include credit card services, the services overlapped; hence, the Board characterized them as “legally identical, in part,” and concluded that factor two weighed heavily in favor of confusion. The Federal Circuit found substantial evidence supported that conclusion and affirmed the Board’s factor‑two analysis.

Second, the TTAB relied on third‑party registrations that covered both credit card services and broader banking/financial services to support the proposition that the two types of services often emanate from a single source. The Board cited examples showing that other firms use Aspire‑formative marks on both types of services—evidence that undercut the inference that the services are distinct in the marketplace.

The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s factor‑two finding, but it also identified a logical inconsistency in the Board’s subsequent analysis of factor six. After determining that the services were highly similar (and thus broadening the class of consumers and channels under consideration), the Board later narrowed its universe of third‑party Aspire marks for factor six to only those identified for “credit card services,” excluding those covering other banking/finance services. The court held that this inconsistency was legal error: the scope used to identify similar third‑party marks should be consistent with the earlier finding of service similarity. Accordingly, the Federal Circuit vacated and remanded the Board’s factor‑six analysis (and, because the strength of CC Serve’s mark and the overall commercial impression of the marks may be affected by factor six, it remanded factor one as well), while affirming factor two.

The lesson from Apex is twofold. First, construing identifications and deciding whether they encompass each other is a textual and evidentiary task the Board and courts will resolve based on the plain meaning of the terms, aided by dictionaries and industry materials; a finding that two identifications are legally identical in part is supported by evidence that a named service (like credit card issuance) falls within the normal ambit of a broader category (like banking). Second, once the Board determines the services overlap broadly, it must apply that same scope when it measures the number and nature of similar marks in use (factor six) and when it assesses the marks’ commercial strength and overall commercial impression (factor one). Inconsistency among those analyses will invite vacatur and remand.

## Practical framework for applying Factor 2 (and linked factors) to new problems

When you confront a new fact pattern involving alleged relatedness of goods or services, proceed in this order:

1. Identify the operative wording. Start with the application’s and registration’s identifications. If either is broad and unrestricted, the default presumptions about channels and consumers apply.

2. Construe the terms. Use dictionaries, statutory/regulatory definitions, trade resources, and the parties’ evidence to determine what the terms would reasonably encompass to an ordinary consumer or a person in the trade.

3. Determine overlap or legal identity. Ask whether one identification literally matches the other, and if not, whether one identification reasonably includes the other (legal identity), or whether the two cover related but distinct services.

4. Apply the presumption of shared channels and consumers if identifications are unrestricted. Seek evidence to rebut the presumption if you want to argue for distinct channels/consumers.

5. Produce third‑party evidence. Collect registrations and (preferably) evidence of third‑party use showing that similar marks are used on the same or related services. Evaluate whether the quantum and quality of third‑party use suggest the cited mark is relatively weak.

6. Maintain consistent scope across DuPont analyses. If you find broad similarity for factor two, use the same service scope when evaluating third‑party evidence under factor six and when assessing the marks’ commercial impressions under factor one.

7. Remember interaction with other factors. Factor two is important but not dispositive. Even when services are legally identical, the Board must weigh other factors—especially the similarity of the marks, the strength of the senior mark, channels and consumers, and any evidence of actual confusion or lack thereof—to reach the ultimate likelihood‑of‑confusion conclusion.

## Concluding observations

The TTAB’s emphasis on identifications in the DuPont analysis is a doctrinal and procedural reality. Applicants and counsel should draft identifications with care: broad wording invites expansive presumptions that can prove fatal in oppositions. Conversely, owners of earlier registrations should be mindful that even narrow service terms can be subsumed within broader descriptions, and that third‑party evidence may narrow the scope of protection. Apex Bank v. CC Serve demonstrates both the power of textual construction (the affirmation of factor two) and the necessity of analytical consistency across factors (the vacation of factor six and factor one where the Board tightened its universe of comparison after finding broad similarity). Mastery of these principles enables better fact development and clearer strategic choices in trademark litigation before the TTAB and on appeal.

# Strength of the Prior Mark and the ‘Crowded Field’ Doctrine (DuPont Factors 5 and 6)

This chapter examines how courts and the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (Board) evaluate the strength of a prior mark and the role of third‑party marks in narrowing a senior registrant’s scope of protection. The analysis is driven by two DuPont factors that frequently determine the outcome of Section 2(d) disputes: (1) conceptual and commercial strength of the prior mark (often treated under the umbrella of the “fifth” DuPont factor or as an aspect of the overall likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis) and (2) the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods or services (the sixth DuPont factor). Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., decided by the Federal Circuit in 2025, provides a useful vehicle for discussing these issues. Apex demonstrates both the utility and the limits of third‑party evidence: third‑party registrations and marketplace uses can—and often should—inform the scope of protection accorded to a prior mark, but they must be evaluated against a properly defined universe of similar goods or services and a consistently applied definition of the relevant consumer class.

## Conceptual strength on the Abercrombie spectrum versus commercial strength from marketplace recognition

Trademarks possess two distinct types of strength that courts weigh when determining the likelihood of confusion: conceptual strength and commercial strength. Conceptual strength is a function of the inherent distinctiveness of a mark independent of marketplace use. The classic way to analyze conceptual strength is the Abercrombie spectrum coined in Abercrombie & Fitch Co. v. Hunting World, Inc.: generic (no protection) → descriptive (protection only with acquired distinctiveness or limited to narrow scope) → suggestive → arbitrary or fanciful (highest inherent strength). A mark that is fanciful or arbitrary (e.g., "Kodak") is conceptually strong and presumptively entitled to a broad scope of protection; a descriptive mark that has not acquired distinctiveness is conceptually weak.

Commercial strength, by contrast, derives from marketplace recognition—consumer awareness, sales, advertising, length of use, and evidence of fame. A conceptually weak mark can acquire commercial strength through extensive use and advertising, and fame can increase a mark’s remedial reach. Courts look for metrics: long and continuous use, large sales volumes, intensive advertising campaigns, high market share, and consumer surveys showing recognition. Survey evidence is especially persuasive because it directly measures the likelihood of consumer confusion and the public’s association of the mark with a single source.

The two dimensions interact. A conceptually strong mark with substantial commercial recognition will enjoy a very broad protection. A conceptually strong but obscure mark enjoys the benefit of inherent distinctiveness but may be less likely to generate confusion in practice if consumers aren’t familiar with it. Conversely, a descriptive mark that has become famous through long use can be afforded broad protection despite its descriptive origins. In practice, courts and the Board often conflate these inquiries because both feed into the overall “commercial impression” inquiry under the first DuPont factor.

Apex Bank illustrates this interplay. CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark is not an invented term, and its conceptual strength is not categorically at the top of the Abercrombie scale; “aspire” is an English verb with ordinary meaning and thus not fanciful. The Board nevertheless concluded that CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark is entitled to a “normal scope of protection accorded inherently distinctive marks” because Apex had not established that the mark was conceptually or commercially weak. That conclusion folded together questions of conceptual distinctiveness and marketplace strength, and it highlights the point that a mark’s placement on the Abercrombie spectrum is only part of the inquiry—the Board must also consider actual marketplace recognition if third‑party evidence suggests the mark is relatively weak in practice.

## What third‑party registrations can and cannot prove

Third‑party trademark registrations (and applications) are commonly introduced in DuPont litigation. They can be useful, but they also have important evidentiary limits.

What registrations can prove:
- Conceptual weakness of a term: A significant number of third‑party registrations incorporating a particular word or element for similar goods/services suggests that the word is in common commercial use and unlikely to serve as a strong source identifier on its own. Courts have treated third‑party registrations as evidence that a word is weak or commonly used in a field, and therefore deserving of a narrower scope of protection (see Palm Bay Imps., Inc. v. Veuve Clicquot Ponsardin Maison Fondee En 1772).
- The existence of a crowded field: Registrations across a range of parties for similar goods can support a conclusion that the marketplace is populated with similar marks.
- The acceptability of coexistence in theory: The fact that the PTO issued registrations for similar marks can be cited to show that similar marks have legally coexisted (but this is only persuasive, not dispositive).

What registrations cannot prove (or prove only weakly):
- Actual marketplace use or consumer recognition: A registration by itself does not demonstrate that consumers actually encountered the mark in the marketplace. The PTO registers marks that may not be in use (or whose use is uncontested), and the registration does not quantify market penetration, advertising, or sales. Relying solely on registrations risks mistaking bureaucratic outcome for market realities.
- Temporal or geographic relevance: Registrations may be abandoned, may postdate priority, or may have limited territorial impact. Registrations filed after the priority date of the senior user cannot show preexisting consumer habituation.
- Legal conclusions: Registrations do not establish that confusion will not occur; the PTO’s allowance of a registration is not binding on courts or the Board in an opposition or cancellation.

Because of these limitations, courts often give stronger weight to registrations that are accompanied by evidence of actual use (screenshots, specimens, sales figures) and to registrations for identical or truly similar goods/services. In short, registrations are helpful but are most persuasive when paired with marketplace evidence.

## What third‑party marketplace use can and cannot prove

Actual marketplace use—advertising, websites, sales, customer testimonials, trade press coverage, distribution networks—is generally the most persuasive evidence of the real commercial environment. Third‑party use can show that consumers routinely encounter multiple similar marks in a field, which supports the “crowded field” argument.

What marketplace use can prove:
- Consumer habituation and ease of distinction: Extensive marketplace use of similar marks teaches consumers to distinguish among them. Courts treating crowded‑field evidence rely on the assumption that repeated exposure trains consumers to pick up on differences and reduces the likelihood of source confusion (see Juice Generation, Inc. v. GS Enters. LLC and Jack Wolfskin Ausrustung Fur Draussen GmbH & Co. KGAA v. New Millennium Sports, S.L.U.).
- Relative weakness of the senior mark in practice: If many others use the same or similar term in commerce for related goods, that tends to show that the term is a common industry term rather than a strong source indicator.
- Channels and consumers: Marketplace use helps define who the relevant consumers are (trade purchasers, sophisticated business customers, mass public) and the typical channels through which they encounter marks.

What marketplace use cannot prove (or proves only weakly):
- Consumer confusion outcomes: Evidence of third‑party marketplace use does not by itself show that consumers will or will not be confused by the particular pair of marks at issue. It is circumstantial and must be tied to the other DuPont factors.
- Comparability without context: A showing that a third party uses an “Aspire” mark for an unrelated line of goods (e.g., educational services) is less relevant than showing use in the same class of services. The similarity of the goods/services is essential to the probative value of marketplace use evidence.
- Indifference to timing and scope: Marketplace use after the critical priority date does not demonstrate preexisting consumer habituation. Also, localized use may not inform a nationwide assessment unless the relevant market is localized.

The practical implication is that litigants should pair registration evidence with contemporaneous specimens of use, advertising metrics, sales data, and consumer-facing materials that demonstrate how often consumers actually see similar marks and in what contexts.

## The crowded field rationale: consumer habituation to similar marks reduces confusion risk

The core policy behind crowdsourcing third‑party evidence is intuitive: when consumers routinely encounter many similar marks in a field, they become accustomed to distinguishing sources and are less likely to be confused by another similar mark. Courts describe this as a reduction in a senior mark’s commercial strength and a narrowing of its scope of protection.

Several Federal Circuit decisions illustrate this point. In Juice Generation, the court considered over two dozen third‑party uses of “Juice” and related terms in the beverage field and concluded that the crowded field reduced the senior mark’s claim to an expansive territorial or product scope. In Jack Wolfskin the court relied on voluminous evidence of paw‑print design elements used in the outerwear market to conclude that the design element's conceptual and commercial strength were attenuated by common use. These cases show that the presence of many similar marks can be powerful evidence that consumers will make distinctions rather than assume a common source.

But the crowded field rationale is not an automatic defense to a senior user’s claim. Courts still ask whether the third‑party marks are sufficiently similar and used on sufficiently similar goods or services to teach consumers to distinguish among them. The strength of a crowded field argument depends on the number of similar marks, their visibility, the closeness of the goods or services, and the sophistication and buying patterns of the relevant consumers.

## Thresholds and labels: ‘considerable’ or ‘ubiquitous’ evidence—no magic numbers

Judges and practitioners often use adjectives—“considerable,” “ubiquitous,” “voluminous”—to describe the quantity of third‑party evidence necessary to show a crowded field. The phrasing in Federal Circuit decisions left some readers hoping for a numeric rule: how many third‑party uses suffice? The short answer is that there is no fixed numerical threshold; context matters.

A few guiding principles emerge from the case law:
- Relative, not absolute: Courts assess the evidence in relation to the size and character of the relevant market. Twenty similar marks in a small, niche industry may be significant; the same number in a vast national market may be less persuasive.
- Quality beats quantity: A smaller number of highly visible competitors (national brands with prominent advertising and retail presence) can be more damaging to a senior mark’s claim than numerous obscure or marginal uses. A single national player with significant market share may reinforce the senior mark’s strength, not weaken it.
- Overlap of goods/services matters: Third‑party uses that are closely aligned with the senior user’s goods or services are far more probative than tangential uses. Courts discount evidence of similar words used in distinct service contexts.
- Consumer experience and sophistication: Where consumers are sophisticated or make careful purchasing decisions (e.g., B2B financial services), smaller amounts of third‑party use may suffice for habituation effects.
- Timing and continuity are important: Evidence must be contemporaneous with the dispute’s relevant priority timeframe to show habituation of consumers, and consistent use over time strengthens the crowded‑field argument.

Practically, litigants and adjudicators should avoid obsession with counts. The question is whether the cumulative record—quantity, quality, visibility, and relevancy of third‑party uses—shows that the marketplace has trained consumers to distinguish among similar marks, thereby narrowing the senior mark’s practical scope.

## Financial services examples: relatedness across credit cards, banking, lending, and fintech

The financial services sector illustrates the subtleties in applying Factor 6. Companies often provide overlapping services under different arrangements, and brands appear across traditional banking, card processing, lending platforms, and fintech apps. This intermixing complicates the assessment of “similar goods/services” and the probative value of third‑party marks.

Key observations for financial services:
- Functional overlap: Credit card programs, payment processing, account servicing, and lending are often integrated by partnerships. A bank may issue a co‑branded card while a fintech processes transactions. Even if different entities perform functions, the consumer often perceives the services as functionally related (e.g., both involve credit, repayment, account management).
- White‑label arrangements: In the credit card world, issuers and program managers commonly white‑label services for banks. A consumer’s card may bear a bank’s brand while the processing and account servicing are performed by a separate company. This blurs the line between the provider of the service and brand identification.
- Distribution channels matter: Some financial services are heavily digital (fintech apps), while others operate through branch banking. Third‑party marks used across different channels—e.g., “Aspire” used both by a credit card program and a mobile banking app—can be persuasive evidence of a crowded field if consumers encounter both offerings in similar contexts (mobile enrollment, app stores).
- Consumer expectations: Financial products often involve considered decisions (reading terms, comparing rates), but many consumers purchase financial services with limited brand attention (e.g., acceptance of a merchant‑provided card). Sophistication varies across products and consumer segments.

In Apex Bank, the Board recognized a high degree of similarity between CC Serve’s “credit card services” and Apex’s “banking and financing services.” Given such factual overlap, evidence of third‑party “Aspire” marks used across banking and card products was especially relevant. The case shows that in finance, labels that appear across product lines and delivery channels are apt to be probative for Factor 6—provided the Board treats them consistently with its earlier finding about relatedness (a point to which we return).

## What third‑party evidence must show: alignment with the relevant consumer and market scope

Two related concepts govern whether a piece of third‑party evidence will be given weight under Factor 6: (1) the relevant consumer class, and (2) the proper scope of goods/services to consider. Both are fact questions informed by the second DuPont factor (similarity of goods/services) and the evidence of marketplace channels.

Define the relevant public: The probative value of third‑party marks depends on which consumers are likely to encounter the marks. If the product is a retail consumer product sold in supermarkets, the relevant public is the general purchasing public; if it’s a specialized service sold to sophisticated purchasers, the relevant public is narrower and more knowledgeable.

Align goods/services across factors: If the Board or court has already found that the parties’ goods or services are highly similar (as happened in Apex with credit card and banking/finance services), then it is inconsistent to narrow the third‑party universe for Factor 6 to a subset (e.g., limiting to only “credit card services”) without explanation. The similarity finding in Factor 2 sets the scope for what counts as “similar goods/services” for purposes of measuring how crowded the field is. Apex Bank stands for the proposition that the analysis must be coherent across factors: you cannot use a wide definition of relatedness to find similarity in Factor 2 and then contract that definition for Factor 6 to exclude many relevant third‑party marks.

Temporal relevance: Third‑party uses must be shown to have existed at a time that could have affected consumer habituation before or at the senior user’s priority date (unless the dispute concerns subsequent issues). Evidence of third‑party uses that postdate the relevant timeframe is less helpful to demonstrate preexisting crowding.

Geographic relevance: Where services are local or regional, third‑party uses confined to distant markets may be less probative.

## Apex Bank’s core lesson: scope of ‘similar goods/services’ under Factor 6 must align with Factor 2 findings

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. provides the clearest recent articulation of a principle that follows logically from DuPont: when a tribunal has already found the parties’ goods or services to be highly similar (or partially legally identical) under the second DuPont factor, it must apply that same definition of similarity when evaluating the field of third‑party marks under the sixth factor. The Board in Apex found that “credit card services” and “banking and financing services” were highly similar; nevertheless, when assessing Factor 6, the Board restricted itself to third‑party marks that explicitly referenced credit card services and excluded many marks using “Aspire” or “Aspire‑formative” elements in broader banking and finance contexts. The Federal Circuit held this to be error—once the Board has accepted a broad scope of similarity, it cannot then narrow the scope for assessing crowding without explanation.

The practical takeaways:
- Coextensive analysis: The universe of third‑party marks relevant under Factor 6 should be at least as broad as the universe of similar goods/services the tribunal found under Factor 2. Otherwise the Board risks undercounting or mischaracterizing evidence of crowding.
- Explain deviations: If the Board narrows the universe for Factor 6 despite a broader Factor 2 finding, it must provide a reasoned explanation—e.g., showing that particular third‑party uses, while arguably related, still differ in channels or consumer base enough to be irrelevant.
- Integrative reasoning: DuPont factors are not siloed. Findings under one factor inform the application of others. The appellate reversal in Apex demonstrates that inconsistent treatment can be reversible error because it distorts the aggregate likelihood‑of‑confusion assessment.

## Practical guidance for practitioners and tribunals

For litigators:
- Collect both registrations and contemporaneous marketplace evidence (screenshots, sales summaries, advertising, app‑store listings, press coverage) showing that third‑party uses were visible to consumers within the relevant timeframe and market.
- Define the relevant public carefully and use evidence of distribution channels and advertising to demonstrate which consumers are likely to encounter competing marks.
- If arguing crowded field, emphasize both number and prominence: a few national players may suffice; many obscure uses may not.
- If the senior user’s mark has been used across related financial services, compile evidence of cross‑use (co‑branding, white‑labeling, partnership disclosures) to show functional relatedness.

For the Board and courts:
- Apply an internally consistent definition of “similar goods/services” across DuPont factors. If Factor 2 finds high similarity, Factor 6 must consider third‑party marks across that same range, unless the tribunal explains why certain uses should be excluded.
- Assess registrations and marketplace use together; registrations without use are weaker evidence of consumer habituation.
- Avoid numeric fixation; instead, explain why the quantity, quality, and visibility of third‑party marks amount to (or fall short of) a “crowded field” given the market at issue.

## Checklist for applying Factor 6 in light of Apex Bank

1. Start from your Factor 2 finding: define the goods/services that you have already found to be similar.
2. Define the relevant consumer class and channels of trade that those consumers use.
3. Collect third‑party evidence that matches the scope from step 1 and the consumer/channel definition from step 2.
4. Evaluate quantity, prominence, timing, and continuity of third‑party uses—give weight to national, longstanding, or heavily advertised uses.
5. Pair registrations with specimens of actual use; evaluate registrations that postdate the critical timeframe with caution.
6. Explain reasoning: if you exclude classes of third‑party marks, provide a fact‑based justification consistent with your Factor 2 analysis.
7. Remember the holistic DuPont inquiry: results on Factor 6 will inform, and be informed by, conclusions on conceptual/commercial strength and the overall commercial impression.

Conclusion

Third‑party registrations and marketplace uses are indispensable tools in modern DuPont analysis, but they are not a panacea. The evidence must be relevant to the proper universe of goods/services and consumers. Apex Bank reinforces that DuPont factors must be applied coherently: findings about product relatedness dictate the breadth of the field the tribunal should examine when assessing crowding and the prior mark’s practical strength. When courts or tribunals keep the analytical frame consistent and evaluate registrations and use together, the crowded‑field doctrine can be applied in a principled way to ensure that the scope of trademark protection reflects both legal distinctiveness and marketplace realities.

# Similarity of the Marks (DuPont Factor 1): Commercial Impression and Context

The first DuPont factor directs courts and the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (the Board) to compare the marks "in their entireties as to appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression." In re E. I. DuPont DeNemours & Co., 476 F.2d 1357, 1361 (C.C.P.A. 1973). That principle is deceptively simple in words and complex in application. This chapter explains how courts apply the entireties rule without improper dissection, how certain elements (generic or descriptive matter, design elements, word order, and dominant components) are weighted, how standard-character registrations expand scope, and how the mark’s conceptual strength (including evidence of third-party uses) interlocks with the comparison under Factor 1. We ground the doctrinal points in the Federal Circuit’s Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. decision and related precedent, and we give practicable rules of decision that exam-takers and practitioners can apply.

## Evaluating marks in their entireties: no dissecting but recognizing dominant elements

The “entireties” principle means examiners and courts must strive to form the overall commercial impression a mark makes on an ordinary consumer, rather than engaging in a formal element-by-element dissection that treats each word or design in isolation. See In re I.AM.Symbolic, LLC, 866 F.3d 1315, 1323 (Fed. Cir. 2017). That said, the doctrine does not prohibit recognizing that one element of a compound mark is the source-identifying or dominant element and therefore merits greater weight in the comparison. The line between prohibited dissection and permissible identification of dominant features is practical and fact-driven.

Permissible focus on a dominant element: Courts often say that consumers typically shorten multiword marks in colloquial use to their most salient or distinctive word. Thus, when a mark is a compound (for example, ASPIRE BANK), the dominant word (ASPIRE) may drive the commercial impression, and courts will give that dominant element more weight in assessing similarity. Recognizing a dominant portion is not the same as comparing only that portion while ignoring context; rather, the court compares the whole mark but accordingly explains why one part is likely to catch a consumer’s attention and dominate recollection. The Federal Circuit made this clear in I.AM.Symbolic: the focus remains on the overall commercial impression, but attention may reasonably center on the portion of the mark that consumers are most likely to remember.

Impermissible dissection: What courts must avoid is artificially parsing marks into neat parts and weighing each as if they were independent words of equal source-identifying force, then mechanically comparing like-to-like. For example, breaking ASPIRE BANK into AS + PIRE + BANK and treating the syllables separately, or comparing only the shared letters without regard to the presence of the generic “BANK” or to any distinctive stylization, would be prohibited dissection. Likewise, isolating a design element from its context and comparing it alone—when the design is not the dominant impression—can produce an erroneous result.

Application in Apex: The Federal Circuit stressed the need to revisit the Board’s Factor 1 analysis because a revised assessment of the mark’s strength (Factor 6) could change the overall commercial impression. The court did not mandate that the Board ignore dominant elements; it required that any weighting of such elements occur within an overall, contextual assessment consistent with the entireties rule.

Practical guidance: On exam or in practice, articulate why a particular component is likely to dominate consumer perception—frequency of use, linguistic prominence, or a descriptive tail that consumers treat as generic. Then explain the whole-mark comparison, explicitly addressing appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression, and showing why the dominant element shifts the balance.

## Weighting descriptive or generic matter (e.g., ‘Bank’) in the overall impression

Generic and highly descriptive matter receives little or no source-indicating weight in a likelihood-of-confusion comparison. If part of the mark will be perceived by consumers as merely describing the goods or the class of provider (e.g., “BANK” for banking services), that matter should be accorded accordingly reduced importance in the Factor 1 analysis.

Legal baseline: Generic terms can never function as trademarks and thus contribute nothing to distinctiveness; descriptive terms are weaker and receive narrower protection. When a composite mark includes a generic or descriptive component, courts will generally discount that component in determining the commercial impression. The Board and courts also give less weight to matter that is commonly used in the trade. For example, “BANK” used in connection with banking and financial services will generally be treated as generic or at least descriptive and given minimal weight when the marks are compared.

How to apply the rule: The analyst should (1) determine whether the element is generic or descriptive, sometimes by reference to dictionary definitions or trade usage; (2) decide how consumers will perceive that element in context; and (3) treat that element as less important in the comparison, while still noting any color, stylization, or placement that might cause consumers to treat the descriptive word as part of a unitary source indicator.

Apex-specific considerations: In Apex Bank, the applicant’s filings claimed the ASPIRE BANK word and design marks for “banking and financing services.” “BANK” is plainly descriptive/generic in that context. Thus "ASPIRE" is likely the dominant source-identifying element. The Board on remand should discount “BANK” and evaluate how consumers will remember and refer to the mark—most likely as “Aspire.” That discounting does not automatically produce a likelihood-of-confusion finding, but it focuses attention on the true source-identifying portion of the mark.

Caveat: Descriptive matter may be stylized or combined with design elements such that, in practice, it creates a distinctive composite. If the design or stylization is sufficiently distinctive and prominent, the descriptive portion may exert less effect on the consumer’s recollection than the design. Always analyze whether the visual or phonetic presentation elevates the descriptive element’s source-identifying potential.

## Standard character marks and scope across stylizations

A standard-character (or word) mark registration protects the mark’s literal word(s) regardless of font, style, size, or color. That breadth is important in Factor 1 comparisons because a registered standard-character mark can be shown in any stylized form in evidence and still be considered the same mark.

Legal effect: When a registrant owns a standard-character registration for, say, ASPIRE, their protection covers the word in any stylization, making it harder for an applicant to rely on differing fonts or design to distinguish its mark unless it can show that the design contributes a materially different overall commercial impression. Conversely, an applicant’s design claim (a word-plus-design mark) may be meaningful if the design is distinctive enough to alter the commercial impression beyond the protected scope of the registrant’s standard-character mark.

Application in Apex: CC Serve owned a standard-character registration for ASPIRE. Apex sought a registration for ASPIRE BANK as a word and design mark. Because CC Serve’s mark is standard-character, the Board must compare Apex’s proposed stylized composite to the registrant’s plain ASPIRE across any stylization. If the design elements of Apex’s mark are minimal or conventional, they will be insufficient to avoid confusion, because the standard-character ASPIRE covers identical word usage in any style. Thus the Board should evaluate whether the design features in Apex’s logo create a distinctive overall impression that would lead consumers to perceive ASPIRE BANK as a different source from ASPIRE.

Practical point: A party defending against a standard-character mark should show that the applicant’s stylization is sufficiently distinctive—through unusual design, color, or arrangement—to change the overall commercial impression. The applicant, conversely, should not assume that stylization alone will defeat a standard-character proprietor’s claim.

## Connotation and suggestiveness in financial services (e.g., aspirational terms like ‘Aspire’)

Connotation—what the mark suggests to an ordinary consumer—is central to the first-factor analysis. Names in the financial services context often operate as suggestive or laudatory terms (e.g., “Aspire,” “Prosper,” “Trust”) and therefore occupy varying positions on the distinctiveness spectrum.

Spectrum placement: Arbitrary or fanciful marks (e.g., made-up words) are the strongest for trademark purposes; suggestive marks (which require consumer imagination to connect to the goods/services) are next strongest; descriptive marks are weaker and generic marks are unprotectable. “Aspire” is typically suggestive or laudatory in the financial-services field: it connotes aspiration, upward mobility, or customer ambition. Because it evokes the character of the user’s goals rather than directly describing a specific financial product, it will often be treated as inherently distinctive (i.e., protectable without proof of acquired distinctiveness), but its conceptual distinctiveness is not absolute—it can be weakened by pervasive third-party use.

How connotation affects Factor 1: A mark with a strong, unique connotation commands broader protection; a suggestive but common term, used widely in the industry, may be less likely to cause confusion when used by different sources. Thus the meaning consumers attach to a word—how ordinary customers interpret “Aspire” in the context of financial services—shapes the entireties analysis.

Apex application: The Board must decide how consumers perceive “Aspire” in the financial-services universe. Do customers see it as a distinctive brand name or as a dictionary-like aspirational verb commonly used in marketing? The answer affects how much weight to place on the common element between CC Serve’s ASPIRE and Apex’s ASPIRE BANK. If “Aspire” has a strong conceptual meaning for consumer identity of source, an “ASPIRE BANK” mark may be more likely to cause confusion; if the term is weak because many banks and card programs use it, the Board should treat it as entitled to a narrow scope of protection.

## Interdependence with mark strength: weaker marks require greater similarity to confuse

The DuPont framework is interdependent: findings under one factor often inform others. Factor 6 (third-party use and registrations) is especially relevant to Factor 1 because evidence that a mark is commercially weak—common in the trade—narrows its scope of protection and means that only highly similar marks will create a likelihood of confusion. Put another way, when a senior mark is weak, an applicant’s mark must be more similar to cause confusion; when the senior mark is strong, even moderate similarity can be confusing.

Doctrinal basis: The Federal Circuit has repeatedly emphasized this interdependence. Evidence of numerous third-party uses of a formative element tends to show that consumers have been “educated” to distinguish between similar marks and that the element is weak as a source identifier. See Palm Bay Imps., Inc. v. Veuve Clicquot Ponsardin Maison Fondee En 1772, 396 F.3d 1369, 1373 (Fed. Cir. 2005); Juice Generation, Inc. v. GS Enters. LLC, 794 F.3d 1334, 1338 (Fed. Cir. 2015).

How that affects analysis: On remand, the Board must not treat Factor 1 in a vacuum. If, after considering all relevant third-party uses (and adopting a scope of similar services consistent with its finding under Factor 2), the Board finds that many financial-services providers use “Aspire” or similar marks, it should construe the commercial impression of the registered ASPIRE as weaker and require a closer similarity for a finding of likelihood of confusion. Conversely, if ASPIRE is relatively unique across the relevant field, that will amplify the significance of a shared element (ASPIRE) in the Factor 1 comparison.

Apex implications: The Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s Factor 6 analysis because the Board limited its universe of third-party marks too narrowly. Because Factor 6 bears directly on how much protection ASPIRE merits, the Factor 1 comparison must be recalibrated on remand in light of a proper evaluation of the third-party usage evidence.

## The I.AM.Symbolic principle: overall commercial impression drives the inquiry

I.AM.Symbolic makes explicit that the central question under Factor 1 is the overall commercial impression made by the marks as experienced in the marketplace. That decision teaches several points: first, the marks are compared as a whole; second, the comparison is contextual—what the consumer sees and expects in the marketplace matters; third, the analysis is not purely lexical but visual and conceptual.

Operationalizing I.AM.Symbolic: To determine the commercial impression courts consider: (1) how the mark looks on packaging, websites, or advertisements; (2) how it sounds when spoken (important for services marketed orally); (3) what the mark connotes to the relevant consumer; and (4) whether the arrangement of words and the presence of design elements create an impression distinct from the literal words. The court should ask: what impression is likely to persist in the mind of the ordinary purchaser who encounters the mark during a typical marketing channel?

Evidence that matters: Actual marketing excerpts, specimens, consumer surveys, trade usage, and even dictionary meanings can inform the commercial-impression inquiry. Where there is a colorable difference in stylization or a striking design element, a specimen showing how the mark is used in commerce will be especially valuable.

Apex guidance: The Federal Circuit recognized that the Board must revisit its Factor 1 assessment because the mark’s commercial impression depends upon the mark’s conceptual strength (Factor 6) and on how consumers will perceive the ASPIRE element across services. Thus, on remand the Board should apply I.AM.Symbolic’s holistic, contextual approach: give appropriate weight to the whole-next-to-whole comparison, but explain the role of particular elements (like “BANK”) in forming the overall impression.

## Apex Bank remand: revisiting Factor 1 after recalibrating mark strength

What precisely must happen on remand? The Federal Circuit’s opinion gives a roadmap. First, the Board must expand the universe of third-party marks it considers under Factor 6 to include marks used on services the Board has already found to be highly similar (not merely identical). Because the Board concluded the parties’ services are highly similar, the Board cannot limit third-party evidence to credit-card-only uses; it must consider all third-party uses of ASPIRE or Aspire-formative marks in banking and broader financial services. That broader review may reveal that ASPIRE is either weak (widely used) or relatively strong (few common uses).

Second, the Board must revisit Factor 1 in light of whatever it finds under Factor 6. If it finds the ASPIRE element commercially weak, the Board should explain why the residual differences—such as the presence of “BANK,” any design elements in Apex’s mark, or differences in trade channels—are sufficient to avoid confusion despite the shared term. If, instead, ASPIRE retains normal or strong protection, the Board should explain why the shared word ASPIRE (likely the dominant element in ASPIRE BANK) creates a likelihood of confusion even if “BANK” is generic.

Third, the Board must provide a reasoned explanation for how each aspect of the marks contributes to the overall commercial impression: appearance (shared word, typography, design), sound (how consumers say “Aspire” and “Aspire Bank”), connotation (aspirational meaning and the likely consumer reaction), and commercial impression (where consumers will encounter the marks—their sophistication, channels, and typical decision-making process).

Practical steps the Board should take on remand:
- Reassess the relevant consumer group and channels of trade consistent with the earlier Factor 2 finding of high similarity in services.
- Re-evaluate the scope and impact of third-party ASPIRE uses on financial services, including registrations, marketplace evidence, and the extent of use (national vs. limited).
- Determine whether “BANK” is generic/descriptive as to Apex’s identified services and reduce its weight accordingly.
- Compare the entire marks—ASPIRE (standard-character) versus ASPIRE BANK (word and design)—considering the standard-character breadth of the registrant’s rights.
- Explain whether and to what extent any design elements in Apex’s proposed mark materially change the commercial impression from ASPIRE alone.
- Tie the analysis to consumer sophistication and purchasing conditions that might mitigate or exacerbate confusion risk.

## Application heuristics and a checklist for Factor 1 analysis

For law students and practitioners, here is a concise checklist to use when preparing or evaluating a Factor 1 analysis:

1. Identify the marks and their registrations (standard-character vs. stylized, word-plus-design).
2. Determine the relevant consumer and channels of trade (use Factor 2 findings to inform this).
3. Ask whether any component is generic or descriptive (e.g., “BANK”) and give it reduced weight.
4. Evaluate appearance, sound, connotation, and overall commercial impression—explain which element(s) are likely dominant and why.
5. Consider the standard-character mark’s scope: does it cover the applicant’s stylization?
6. Review evidence of third-party use/registration (Factor 6) and assess how that evidence affects conceptual strength. Adjust the required degree of similarity accordingly: weaker senior marks require closer similarity.
7. Analyze whether the combined mark (word + design) creates a materially different commercial impression than the senior mark alone.
8. State a reasoned conclusion that integrates all aspects and explains the likely reaction of an ordinary purchaser.

Illustrative hypotheticals:
- “ASPIRATION BANK” vs. registered “ASPIRE”: If many banks use “aspire” or “aspiration” in marks, ASPIRE is weak—mere addition of “BANK” probably unlikely to cause confusion unless the stylization is identical or other factors (channel of trade, consumer sophistication) point to confusion.
- Stylized logo “Aspire” with unique swoosh vs. standard-character “ASPIRE”: if the logo is prominent and used consistently in advertising, it may tilt the commercial impression away from the plain ASPIRE; however, a standard-character registrant can still assert rights against highly similar stylizations because its registration covers the word across styles.

Conclusion

Factor 1 requires a holistic, contextual comparison that looks at how ordinary consumers perceive the marks in the marketplace. Courts must avoid impermissible dissection, but they may and should explain why certain elements dominate the commercial impression. Generic or descriptive elements like “BANK” receive less weight, and standard-character registrations carry broad scope across stylizations. Crucially, the mark-strength evidence gathered under Factor 6 is not an add-on but a determinant of how much similarity is needed to cause confusion; a weak mark narrows protection and demands closer similarity for confusion to exist. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. illustrates these interdependencies: because the Board limited the universe of third-party marks improperly, its Factor 6 finding could not stand, and the Factor 1 analysis—dependent on the mark’s strength—must be revisited. On remand, a careful, contextual explanation that follows the checklist above should produce a reasoned, reviewable decision.

# Consistency Across Factors: Avoiding Scope Mismatches

Trademark likelihood-of-confusion analyses under Section 2(d) of the Lanham Act are multi-factor inquiries. The DuPont framework supplies up to thirteen factors, but courts and tribunals consider only those that are relevant to the case. Although the factors are often analyzed discretely, they do not function in isolation. The critical methodological obligation for adjudicators and advocates is coherence: once the tribunal adopts a scope for the marketplace or a definition of similarity for goods or services, that scope should inform all interrelated DuPont factors unless the record supplies a clearly articulated reason for treating a later factor differently. The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful and recent illustration of this principle. There, the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board found the parties’ services to be highly similar (indeed partially legally identical) under the second DuPont factor but then narrowed the universe of third‑party marks it considered under the sixth DuPont factor to a subset that fit a more limited view of similarity. The Federal Circuit held that narrowing was error and vacated the Board’s sixth- and first-factor findings for reconsideration. This chapter explains why that error matters doctrinally, how it arises in practice, and how practitioners and tribunals can structure analyses to avoid it.

## Principle of analytical consistency: same marketplace scope should inform interrelated factors

At its core, the DuPont inquiry asks whether consumers are likely to confuse the origin of goods or services offered under the competing marks. That question necessarily depends on a factual determination about the marketplace: what products or services are at issue; how they are marketed; who the buyers are; and where and how consumers encounter the marks. The Board’s factual determinations about these matters—particularly determinations that certain goods or services are legally identical or highly similar—are findings that shape the relevant universe for other DuPont factors. A hallmark rule of sound adjudication is that those factual determinations should be carried forward unless there is independent record support to the contrary.

Apex Bank makes this point succinctly. The Board decided that CC Serve’s “credit card services” and Apex’s “banking and financing services” were highly similar and even partly legally identical. That factual determination defined a broad universe of financial-services consumers who might encounter both marks in related commercial contexts. Despite that, the Board limited its sixth-factor analysis (evidence of third‑party use of similar marks) to marks specifically identifying credit-card services, excluding marks that used “Aspire” in connection with other banking and financing services. The Federal Circuit held that this restriction was inconsistent with the Board’s earlier finding and therefore legally erroneous. Put differently, a tribunal cannot fairly claim a broad overlap of services for one factor and then adopt a narrower conception of the marketplace for another factor unless it explains, on the record, why the narrower conception is separately warranted.

This requirement for consistency serves several functions. It avoids internal contradictions in legal reasoning; it ensures that different DuPont factors—many of which depend on the same underlying marketplace facts—are evaluating the same commercial reality; and it produces predictable, administrable outcomes. When a tribunal treats the same connection between goods or services as broad for one factor and narrow for another, the result is an apples-to-oranges comparison that may distort the ultimate likelihood-of-confusion conclusion.

## How a narrow view under Factor 6 can conflict with a broad view under Factor 2

Factor 2, which examines the similarity of goods or services, is often the linchpin of the DuPont analysis. A finding that services are “related in some manner” or that the “circumstances surrounding their marketing” could cause confusion establishes the relevant market overlap. That same overlap determines the relevant class of third‑party marks to be considered under Factor 6, which asks whether a crowded field of similar marks indicates that a particular mark is weak (entitling it to narrower protection).

A narrow Factor 6 inquiry can distort the analytical picture when the tribunal has already adopted a broad Factor 2 finding. Suppose, as in Apex Bank, the Board concludes that credit card services and banking services are highly similar because banks often issue credit cards and the services are marketed to substantially overlapping consumers. If Factor 6 then considers only marks expressly registered for “credit card services,” it disregards similar uses of the same formative in other banking or financing services that are part of the same marketplace. By excluding those uses, the tribunal artificially reduces the apparent frequency of third‑party use, exaggerating the mark’s commercial strength and narrowing the factual basis for the crowded‑field defense. The Federal Circuit recognized this very dynamic and explained that Factor 6 requires consideration of similar marks on similar goods and services—not only identical ones—so a prior finding of substantial overlap in Factor 2 should ordinarily guide the universe considered under Factor 6.

Beyond doctrinal consistency, the practical consequence of such a mismatch is substantive: an improperly narrow Factor 6 analysis can skew the overall likelihood-of-confusion outcome by overstating the senior user’s exclusivity or the junior user’s potential for confusion. The first DuPont factor—similarity of the marks as to appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression—also depends on the mark’s commercial strength. If the Board understates the extent of third‑party use (i.e., the mark’s weakness), it may mischaracterize the mark’s commercial impression and therefore reach an erroneous conclusion about how consumers will perceive the competing marks.

The error identified in Apex Bank is thus not an abstruse methodological quibble; it goes to the heart of how the DuPont factors interlock. Treating similarity as broad for purposes of identifying the relevant consumer group and narrow for collecting third‑party evidence undermines the integrity of the multi-factor test.

## Practical approach: set the scope once, then carry it through

The simplest and most reliable method to avoid scope inconsistencies is to set a clear marketplace scope at the outset of the analysis and apply that scope consistently across interrelated factors. That does not mean a tribunal may never refine its understanding later; it does mean that any refinement must be explained and supported by the record.

A pragmatic seven-step process will help triers of fact and advocates maintain consistency:

1. Define the goods/services precisely. Start by identifying the parties’ claimed classes of goods or services according to the registrations and applications. Use the language in the identification but also analyze what colloquial and industry usage those terms encompass.

2. Decide whether the goods/services are identical, legally identical, or similar. Consider statutory identifications, actual use, channels of trade, overlapping customers, and industry practice. If a tribunal finds “highly similar” or “legally identical” relationships, that finding should be treated as the baseline for related factors.

3. Fix the relevant consumer universe and commercial channels. Once similarity is determined, specify the relevant consumers (e.g., “consumers of banking and credit card services” rather than a narrower subset) and the places they encounter the marks (online portals, bank branches, co‑branded cards issued by partner banks, etc.).

4. Collect third‑party evidence consistent with that scope. For Factor 6, identify third‑party marks used in connection with all services that the tribunal has found to be similar. Include registrations, marketplace examples, and evidence of consumer recognition across the same channels.

5. Apply interrelated factors using the same scope. When evaluating the appearance, sound, and commercial impression of the marks (Factor 1), the fame/strength of the prior mark (Factor 9), channels of trade (Factor 3), conditions of purchase (Factor 11), and consumer sophistication (Factor 13), use the previously defined consumer universe and channels as the common frame.

6. If narrowing or broadening is necessary, explain why. If the record later reveals that a subset of services is meaningfully distinct (for example, different regulatory regimes or distinct customer classes with separate decision-making processes), the tribunal should articulate that evidence and explain why a narrower or broader scope is warranted for the particular factor.

7. Revisit totality-of-the-circumstances with awareness of interdependence. The final likelihood‑of‑confusion determination should reflect the integrated impact of factors analyzed within a consistent marketplace frame.

Advocates can employ the same approach offensively. A plaintiff seeking broad protection should develop the record to support a broad similarity finding at Factor 2 (market studies, overlap in distribution, typical co‑branding relationships). A defendant seeking to show weakness under Factor 6 should gather third‑party registrations and marketplace examples across the same service classes and channels identified in Factor 2.

## Impacts on other factors: channels of trade, consumer sophistication, conditions of purchase

A consistent scope decision will affect many DuPont factors, not just Factors 1, 2, and 6. Three frequently interrelated factors are channels of trade, consumer sophistication/conditions of purchase, and commercial strength/fame.

Channels of trade (often considered under DuPont Factor 3) are central to the likelihood-of-confusion calculus because overlapping channels increase the risk that consumers will encounter both marks. If the tribunal finds, under Factor 2, that banking and credit card services are highly similar and typically sold through the same channels (e.g., online account portals, bank branches, co-branding with retailers), that finding must inform the analysis of trade channels. A later finding that restricts the channels considered—say, by focusing only on the niche channels used exclusively for one party—creates an analytical inconsistency and may underestimate the true overlap in consumer exposure.

Conditions of purchase and consumer sophistication (typically analyzed under DuPont Factors 11 and 13) are likewise affected by scope. If the relevant consumer class is the general public acquiring everyday financial services, the tribunal might conclude that consumers are price- or brand-conscious and not highly sophisticated, which favors a likelihood of confusion. If a tribunal starts with that broad consumer group under Factor 2, it cannot later characterize the relevant purchasers as sophisticated specialists under another factor without record support. Conversely, where the record shows that particular services are purchased after careful consideration (for example, bespoke commercial lending for sophisticated corporate clients), a narrower finding about purchaser sophistication may be justified—but again, the tribunal must explain and ground that narrower view.

Commercial strength and fame (DuPont Factor 9) depend on both the extent of third‑party use and consumer recognition in the relevant market. In Apex Bank, the Board’s limited consideration of third‑party uses for Factor 6 led it to conclude that CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark was entitled to “normal scope” protection. If the Board had included additional third‑party examples in the broader banking-and-finance universe, it might have concluded the mark was conceptually or commercially weaker, changing the weight of Factor 1 and the ultimate outcome. Thus, the initial scope choice directly influences whether a mark is treated as strong or weak.

In short, scope choice is not a technicality; it is the lens through which many DuPont factors are viewed. An inconsistent lens produces inconsistently supported findings that can be—and, as Apex Bank shows, will be—reversed on appeal.

## Using Apex Bank to design a replicable, consistent factor analysis template

Apex Bank gives a clear remedial blueprint: where a tribunal has made a determinative factual finding about similarity of services, it must use that factual baseline when evaluating other interrelated factors, unless an evidentiary reason compels a different approach. Below is a replicable template—derived from Apex Bank and practical adjudicative practice—that practitioners and tribunals can use to frame and execute a consistent DuPont analysis.

1. Opening statement of market scope. Begin the opinion or brief with a short, explicit statement: “For purposes of this analysis, the relevant market comprises [precise list of goods/services], and the relevant consumers are [description].” If the record provides no precise evidence of consumer segmentation, state that the parties’ identifications control.

2. Factor 2 first. Analyze the similarity or dissimilarity of the goods/services thoroughly and explain why the court finds them identical, legally identical in part, highly similar, or dissimilar. Record the conclusion in a way that can be referenced—for example: “Finding: services are highly similar and marketed to the same consumer base.”

3. Carry the finding forward. Use that finding verbatim or reference it by name when analyzing Factors 1, 3, 6, 9, 11, and 13. For example: “Because the services are highly similar (see supra Factor 2), the relevant universe for assessing third‑party use under Factor 6 includes marks used in connection with all banking and financing services—not only credit-card services.”

4. Factor-specific tailoring with explanation. If a factor requires a finer-grained approach (for instance, evidence shows that one category is typically sold only to corporate clients while another is consumer-facing), explain the record basis for that narrower application before applying the narrower scope. Do not simply shift scope for convenience or because one factor might produce a preferred legal result.

5. Consistent evidentiary collection. Ensure that exhibits, third‑party registrations, survey instruments, and witness testimony introduced for one factor would also be considered for related factors unless rebutted. When admitting or citing third‑party evidence for Factor 6, make clear the criteria used to select that evidence (matching service descriptions, overlapping channels of trade, etc.).

6. Conclude with integrated reasoning. End with an integrated “totality of circumstances” discussion that explicitly ties together the findings under the interrelated factors and explains how the consistent scope led to the ultimate conclusion.

7. Preserve appellate record. On appeal, courts review factual findings for substantial evidence and legal conclusions de novo. A well-documented record that shows consistent application of scope reduces the risk of reversal by making clear that factual findings were neither arbitrary nor internally inconsistent.

Apex Bank shows the consequence of failing to follow such a template: the Board’s inconsistent scopes produced error requiring vacatur and remand. By contrast, a clear initial scope and a discipline to carry it through the analysis create defensible, durable decisions.

## When deviations are justified, explain and record them

The requirement of consistency is robust but not absolute. There are legitimate circumstances where different factors reasonably require different scopes. For example, a third‑party use in one narrow segment may inform the relative weakness of a mark in that segment even if the marks are not used in another, broader segment; or empirical evidence might show that certain channels are functionally separate markets with different buyer behavior. The key is transparency: any deviation from the baseline scope must be grounded in evidence and reasoned explanation.

If a tribunal concludes that a narrower scope is appropriate for Factor 6 despite a broader Factor 2 finding, it should state the grounds: perhaps consumer surveys show no cross‑recognition between the sub-segments, or industry witness testimony establishes that firm boundaries exist between the services at issue. Without such an explanation, the decision risks appellate reversal as in Apex Bank.

## Practical takeaways for litigants and examiners

- Litigants should build the record on marketplace overlap early. If you want a broad scope, collect evidence of co‑branding, joint ventures, shared distribution channels, consumer-facing materials showing overlap, and third‑party uses across the related services. If you seek a narrow scope, collect contrary evidence: segregated channels, distinct consumer classes, and purchasing practices showing little or no overlap.

- Examiners and TTAB judges should state their baseline marketplace finding at the outset of the opinion and reference it throughout. Doing so reduces the need for later justificatory gymnastics and helps appellate courts review for substantial evidence.

- When using third‑party registrations and marketplace evidence, collect and analyze examples consistent with the pre-stated scope. Avoid cherry-picking evidence that aligns with an inconsistent or shifting conception of similarity.

- Finally, litigants should anticipate that inconsistent applications of scope will be noticed and corrected on appeal; the appellate courts are particularly sensitive to internal mismatches among DuPont factor findings because those mismatches often signal underlying analytical error.

## Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a practical reminder that DuPont factors are interrelated and that a tribunal’s factual determination about the similarity of goods or services creates an analytic frame that should inform the other, related factors. A consistent approach—define the relevant marketplace early, gather evidence across that marketplace, and apply that scope coherently across Factors 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 11, and 13—promotes reasoned decision-making and reduces reversible error. Where deviation from the baseline is appropriate, articulate the evidentiary basis clearly. Following these rules will lead to analyses that are both persuasive and durable.

# Evidence in TTAB Proceedings: Building and Attacking the Record

Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) proceedings are in many respects contests of proof. Success or failure often turns on how well a party builds a record to support findings under the DuPont likelihood‑of‑confusion framework and how skillfully the opposing party undermines that same record. The Federal Circuit’s opinion in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., 2025 (Fed. Cir. No. 2023‑2143), provides a useful touchstone: the Board’s ultimate ruling turned as much on its evaluation of documentary evidence — what it considered and what it excluded — as on the legal tests. This chapter treats the types of evidence the TTAB will accept, the limits on their probative value, and the procedural tools practitioners should use (and not rely upon) while making and attacking the record. Practical steps and traps to avoid are emphasized throughout.

## Acceptable evidence: dictionary definitions, industry publications, websites, archived pages

The TTAB commonly relies on contemporaneous printed materials to define ordinary meanings and trade usage. Dictionary definitions, industry glossaries, and authoritative treatises are often the simplest way to establish the ordinary meaning or scope of a word in an identification of goods or services. In Apex, the Board consulted dictionary definitions for “banking,” “bank,” and “finance” when construing the parties’ respective identifications; that is a textbook example of how definitions help the Board determine whether services are similar for DuPont factor two.

Industry publications and trade press are particularly valuable when the dispute turns on how an industry uses a term or how services are marketed. Trade journals, banking or payments industry reports, and vendor materials can show that consumers or businesses treat two services as part of the same product ecosystem (for example, that banks and credit‑card program managers are commonly associated in the marketplace). Such evidence is often more persuasive than dictionaries because it shows usage in context rather than abstract meaning.

Websites and web pages are routinely used to show real‑world relationships among services, branding practice, and channels of trade. The TTAB accepts website printouts and online materials subject to proper authentication. Screenshots must be accompanied by either testimony or a declaration that establishes the page, the date it was viewed, and the source. The Wayback Machine and other internet archives are powerful tools for showing historical use because they preserve page content at a given date; courts and the TTAB have accepted Wayback captures when properly authenticated. Apex’s record included website evidence and third‑party material that the Board used to assess relatedness; the control and dating of webpages proved important.

Practical pointers:
- Whenever you rely on online material, capture and preserve the page with metadata (URL, capture date, and the archived image). File an affidavit or declaration of the person who captured the page describing when and how it was obtained and identifying the source.
- Use corroborating sources (e.g., press releases, trade publication coverage, or corporate filings) to confirm claims made on websites, which might be outdated or incomplete.
- Remember that stale or undated webpages are vulnerable to credibility challenges; always anchor web evidence to an authenticated date.

## Third‑party registrations and applications: probative value and limits

Third‑party registration evidence is one of the most frequently used tools to show conceptual or commercial weakness in a mark. The basic idea is that if many different parties are using or have registered variations of a term for similar goods or services, consumers are more likely to be adept at distinguishing among similar marks, and the claimant’s mark is entitled to a narrower scope of protection. The Federal Circuit articulated these principles in Palm Bay and subsequent cases; the Board relied on third‑party registrations in Apex to support relatedness.

But there are three important limits to their probative value that practitioners must keep in mind:

1. Registrations do not prove marketplace use. A live federal registration establishes only that the registered party secured a federal registration; it does not prove actual use in commerce at relevant times. If the opponent wants to show that a third party was using a mark in commerce such that consumers were exposed to it, the registrant or other evidence of use is needed. Because of this, a pile of registrations without proof of use is less persuasive on commercial weakness than registrations coupled with specimens, advertising materials, or sales figures.

2. The relevance of a registration depends on the goods/services identified. Registrations are probative only insofar as their identifications overlap with the goods or services at issue. If a registration covers “financial consulting” and the case concerns “credit card services,” the registration is relevant only if the Board can reasonably deem those services similar or within the same channels of trade. The Apex decision underlines this point: the Board initially limited its review of third‑party marks to those explicitly identifying credit card services; the Federal Circuit found that limitation legally flawed because the Board had already found broader similarity between “banking/finance” and “credit card services.”

3. Quantity and quality matter. A handful of third‑party registrations for a conceptually similar mark is not the same as voluminous evidence of third‑party use and registration. Cases that find weak marks typically show many examples of active commercial use or registrations in the same field. The Board in Apex found only nine Aspire‑formative marks for credit card services and deemed that insufficient to show pervasive use; that quantitative judgment illustrates the distinction.

How to present third‑party registration evidence effectively:
- Use the USPTO’s TSDR records to show registration dates and current status; include screenshots of TSDR pages accompanied by a declaration authenticating the capture.
- Where possible, supplement registrations with evidence of commercial use—specimens, website captures showing the mark in use, sales or advertising materials, and press coverage.
- Organize registrations by timing and by the overlap of identifications with the parties’ services; show why each registration is relevant to the DuPont factor at issue.

## Proving marketplace use: screenshots, articles, consumer reviews, corporate filings

When the Board must evaluate commercial strength, channels of trade, or consumer exposure, proof of marketplace use is often decisive. Proof can take many forms, but each form has authentication and foundation requirements.

Screenshots and web captures. As noted above, screenshots must be authenticated. TTAB practice commonly uses:
- Declarations under oath by a custodian or by counsel’s client who retrieved the pages, stating the URL, the date retrieved, and how the pages were captured;
- Wayback or other archived captures showing the page content on specific dates, authenticated with a declaration from the person who captured the archival image; and
- Records of domain registration or hosting that corroborate the timing and ownership of the page.

Trade and news articles. Trade press and newspaper coverage can demonstrate the marketplace presence and consumer awareness of a brand. Such articles should be filed as printed publications under notice of reliance or introduced through testimony (so as to permit cross‑examination). The more prominent the source and the closer the date to the priority window, the stronger the evidence of consumer exposure.

Consumer reviews and social media. Reviews and social media posts can be persuasive on consumer recognition and reputation, but they are highly fact‑specific and readily challenged on authenticity and representativeness. Use them in combination with other evidence and authenticate through declarations describing the method of capture and the provenance of user posts.

Corporate filings and business registrations. SEC filings, state corporate filings, and official business registrations can show the identity of a mark owner, the dates of business operations, and the nature of goods or services sold. Financial statements, investor presentations, and sales reports (when available) provide especially persuasive proof of commercial activity and consumer reach. For private companies, affidavits from corporate officers with supporting documents can serve the same function.

Practice points on authenticity and admissibility:
- Use notice of reliance (37 C.F.R. §2.122(d)) for printed publications, third‑party registrations, and official records when permissible; when relying on documents through notice of reliance, include a concise explanation of the source and date.
- When possible, present critical marketplace evidence through witness testimony (declaration or deposition) to avoid hearsay challenges and to permit cross‑examination.
- Build redundancy: corroborate website claims with press coverage, industry reports, and third‑party citations to reduce vulnerability to credibility attacks.

## Letters of protest during examination: purpose and effect

Letters of protest are a tool available during ex parte examination; they allow third parties to bring evidence of confusing similarity, lack of use, or other issues to an examining attorney’s attention before publication or registration. The TTAB and the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office often receive such letters, and they can be influential in prosecution. In Apex, CC Serve submitted a letter of protest during prosecution, but the examining attorney nevertheless allowed Apex’s ASPIRE BANK marks to publish for opposition. That sequence illustrates several points about the role and limits of letters of protest.

Purpose. A letter of protest permits a third party to present documentary evidence to the examining attorney (for example, proof of priority, third‑party registrations, or evidence of public confusion) so that the examiner can consider the matter in the initial examination. Letters of protest can be cost‑effective to bring issues to the agency’s attention early in the process.

Limits. Letters of protest do not adjudicate rights between private parties. They are not a substitute for an opposition or cancellation action. An examining attorney may consider and act upon the evidence in the protest, but the PTO is under no obligation to grant the protest, and the letter does not create a record before the TTAB in an inter partes proceeding unless it is introduced there. Apex shows that an applicant can publish a mark despite a protest and still face a successful opposition later on.

Practical advice:
- Use letters of protest to preserve resources and to flag problems during prosecution, but be prepared to file an opposition if the application proceeds to publication.
- When drafting a protest, include concise, well‑documented, contemporaneous evidence (TSDR records, screenshots, press coverage) and explain why the evidence is relevant to registrability.
- If the protest produces no action and the application publishes, promptly evaluate the need for an opposition and consider preserving the protest materials for use in that later proceeding.

## Intent‑to‑use applications and their implications for scope and timing

Intent‑to‑use (ITU) applications under Section 1(b) of the Lanham Act are common for businesses that want to secure priority for a mark they intend to use. ITU filings, however, carry special implications for TTAB proceedings that every practitioner must understand.

Priority and constructive use. An ITU filing alone does not equate to actual use in commerce; registration is not issued on an ITU application until the applicant files a verified statement of use (SOU) or obtains an extension and proves use. That means an ITU applicant does not automatically have the same rights as a registrant who has used the mark. In oppositions under Section 2(d), priority is typically established by either earlier use in commerce or an earlier registration. A registered prior user (like CC Serve in Apex, whose ASPIRE registration dated to 1998) holds established rights that an ITU applicant cannot defeat simply by having filed an ITU application later. Thus, ITU filings can be attacked on the ground that the applicant does not yet have use or priority.

Constructive nationwide priority upon registration. If the ITU applicant ultimately proves use and obtains registration, the applicant may enjoy constructive nationwide priority as of the registration date (or in certain circumstances the filing date). But this valuable protection only arises with successful prosecution to registration. During prosecution and pending opposition, the applicant’s rights remain limited.

Implications for evidence. When opposing an ITU application, the opponent should emphasize its earlier priority (registration or use) and present evidence that the applied‑for services are related to the opponent’s services under DuPont factor two. The ITU applicant must be prepared to show that it is working toward bona fide use and to marshal evidence of intent and subsequent use when required. In Apex, Apex Bank’s ITU application did not insulate it from opposition by CC Serve — the Board evaluated relatedness and likelihood of confusion on the merits while Apex still lacked registration.

Strategic considerations:
- If you represent an ITU applicant, start collecting use evidence early (specimens, website captures, advertising) so that you can establish use if needed and to rebut assertions of relatedness and likely confusion.
- Opponents should exploit the temporal gap between ITU filing and actual use to press the priority and relatedness arguments based on established registrations and marketplace presence.

## Trademark ID Manual: role in construing identifications; effect of entry deletions

The Trademark ID Manual (ID Manual) is the PTO’s list of pre‑approved identifications of goods and services that an examining attorney can accept without further parsing. The ID Manual streamlines prosecution because it offers standard language that the Office treats as acceptable. But when an entry is deleted from the ID Manual or when an examining attorney must construe an identification that is not a precise ID Manual entry, evidence becomes necessary to define the scope of the goods or services.

Role in TTAB analysis. When interpreting an application or registration, the Board gives weight to the ID wording. If the parties’ identifications align with ID Manual entries, the Board will often rely on the ordinary meaning articulated therein. Apex shows the flip side: the entry for “credit card services” had been deleted from the ID Manual after CC Serve’s registration, and the Board needed to define what that phrase encompassed. The Board did so by consulting dictionaries and industry evidence, concluding that the phrase included issuing cards, account management, and related activities. That conclusion then shaped the DuPont analysis.

Effect of entry deletions. When the PTO deletes an entry, the absence does not leave the identifications undefined; it simply requires examiners and the TTAB to construe the terms from other evidence — dictionaries, industry sources, registrations, and marketplace materials. Practitioners should be proactive: when a deletion affects a contested identification, submit evidence and argument defining the ordinary understanding of the term and mapping how services overlap.

Practical rules:
- Don’t assume an ID Manual entry will be dispositive; be ready to defend or clarify the meaning of an identification with contemporary sources.
- If you sign a consent or file an amendment to the identification, be very careful to preserve the scope you intend; later litigation may hinge on fine differences in phraseology.

## Best practices: organizing evidence by factor and ensuring completeness of the TTAB record

The DuPont framework is the organizing principle for the TTAB’s likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis, and a successful evidentiary strategy maps evidence to the specific DuPont factors. Two themes run through best practice advice: (1) organize evidence so the Board can easily connect it to the factor it is intended to prove, and (2) eliminate weaknesses in your evidentiary foundation before they become substantive problems.

Organize evidence by DuPont factor. Create an evidentiary binder (electronic or printed) with tabs for each relevant DuPont factor. Under factor two (relatedness of goods/services), include third‑party registrations that show services offered under similar marks, website captures showing co‑marketing of services, and industry treatises. For factor six (third‑party use/number of similar marks), compile registrations and, crucially, proof of commercial use for each registered mark. For factor one (similarity of marks), include specimens showing how the mark appears in commerce, advertising, and trade dress information. Apex demonstrates why consistent scope across factors is important: the Board’s separate treatment of similarity for the sixth factor — applying a narrower conception than it used for factor two — was reversible error.

Authenticate aggressively. Anticipate authentication challenges to web evidence and archival captures. File affidavits or declarations that explain the capture process, identify custodians of records, and confirm dates. When you rely on third‑party registrations, provide TSDR screenshots showing current status and registration dates. When relying on business filings, use certified copies when possible.

Use testimony strategically. Where critical facts hinge on credibility or detailed explanation (e.g., the scope of an applicant’s marketing program, sales volumes, or the sophistication of purchasers), present witness testimony either through declaration or live/cross‑examinable deposition. Testimony permits cross‑examination, which the Board values.

Avoid surprises; build the record during prosecution or early in the opposition. Letters of protest can be useful but are no substitute for producing your evidence in a TTAB proceeding. Use discovery to obtain specimens of use, advertising spend data, and other materials that reveal the breadth of exposure.

Create a timeline and priority chart. For every opposition, produce a timeline that ties the filing and registration dates to marketplace evidence and shows which uses predated which filing dates. In Apex, the timing of CC Serve’s 1996 priority and Apex’s 2019 ITU filing is a central organizing fact; an opposite strategy would be useless without dates.

Checklist of items to include in an opposition or defense file:
- TSDR records for all cited registrations and applications; screenshots and printed copies with source metadata.
- Website captures (live and archived), with declarations authenticating capture dates and methods.
- Trade press coverage, industry reports, and dictionaries establishing meanings and use.
- Specimens of use, advertising samples, and sales/marketing data to show commercial strength, channels of trade, and consumer exposure.
- Declarations from corporate officers, marketing officers, or third‑party witnesses addressing use, channels of trade, and target customers.
- Consumer survey evidence when appropriate, methodologically sound and submitted with expert witness support.
- A clear index tying each piece of evidence to the specific DuPont factor(s) it is meant to support.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a reminder that the TTAB’s determinations often turn on the texture of documentary proof and the scope of the record. Practitioners who anticipate the Board’s need to define identifications, who authenticate and date web and archival materials, and who understand the probative limits of registrations and protests will be far better positioned to win. Build the record with DuPont in mind, authenticate aggressively, and use the PTO’s procedural tools (not as shortcuts but as complements) to secure the evidence you need for trial.

# Case Study: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

This chapter uses the Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. to illustrate how the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) and reviewing courts apply the multifactor DuPont likelihood-of-confusion analysis. The opinion presents a clean example of (1) how the Board may find high similarity or partial legal identity of services under the second DuPont factor; (2) why consistency in defining the “relevant” services and consumers matters when assessing other DuPont factors; and (3) how third‑party use evidence bears on a mark’s commercial strength under the sixth DuPont factor. We also consider standards of review and practical lessons for trademark practitioners in the financial-services field.

## Factual setup: credit card services versus planned online banking under ASPIRE BANK

At the center of this dispute are two parties operating in the financial-services space and competing over the right to use ASPIRE-formative trademarks. CC Serve owns a federal registration, issued in 1998 and with a priority date in 1996, for the standard-character mark ASPIRE covering “credit card services.” CC Serve’s business model is not a typical retail bank: it partners with banks so that the bank issues ASPIRE-branded credit cards and accounts, but CC Serve and affiliates provide substantial program-management services—servicing accounts, processing transactions, monitoring, and other credit‑card‑related functions. Apex Bank is a brick-and-mortar and retail bank that does not at the time offer credit cards. Apex sought federal registration, via intent‑to‑use applications, for the ASPIRE BANK word and design marks for “banking and financing services,” intending to use the marks for an internet banking brand.

The parties’ applications and the record produced two important initial facts. First, CC Serve’s registration predates Apex’s filings by many years, giving CC Serve the presumptive priority to oppose Apex’s marks. Second, Apex’s identified services—“banking and financing services”—overlap with the range of activities the Board concluded fall within the ordinary meaning of “credit card services” (issuing cards, processing transactions, account management, credit counseling, etc.). Those facts frame the DuPont inquiry: priority favors CC Serve; the central question is whether Apex’s ASPIRE BANK marks would likely confuse the ordinary consumer as to source given the parties’ overlapping services and the marketplace use of “Aspire” by other entities.

## Factor 2 affirmed: high similarity and partial legal identity of services

The second DuPont factor asks whether the parties’ goods or services are related such that concurrent use of similar marks would create a likelihood of confusion. This inquiry does not require literal identity; instead courts and the TTAB ask whether the services are “related in some manner and/or the circumstances surrounding their marketing are such that they could give rise to the mistaken belief that they emanate from the same source.” That legal standard—derived from long-standing precedent such as Coach Services Inc. v. Triumph Learning LLC—focuses on the nature of the services and how they are presented to consumers.

In Apex, the Board carefully parsed the ordinary meanings of the parties’ pleaded identifications of services. Because the Trademark ID Manual entry for “credit card services” had been removed after CC Serve’s registration, the Board examined the descriptive scope of both parties’ services. It concluded that “credit card services” includes not only the issuance of cards but also account management, processing of transactions, credit monitoring, and related financial-account services—all activities that overlap with Apex’s broadly phrased “banking and financing services.” Given that overlap, the Board found the services to be “highly similar” and “legally identical, in part.” The Federal Circuit affirmed that conclusion, emphasizing two propositions students should internalize.

First, an applicant’s and registrant’s services need not be identical for the second DuPont factor to weigh in favor of confusion; analogous or complementary functions suffice. Financial services frequently illustrate that point: credit cards, deposit accounts, loan servicing, and payment processing are distinct service labels but occupy the same marketplace space and are frequently offered under a single brand by banks or financial‑service conglomerates. The Board’s exercise of statutory interpretation of the listed services—reading “banking,” “finance,” and “credit card services” in light of commercial reality—was found by the Federal Circuit to be supported by substantial evidence.

Second, the breadth of a party’s identification of services matters. When a registration or application uses broad terms (e.g., “banking and financing services”) the Board may reasonably read those terms to encompass a range of activities, including services one party believes are outside the other party’s scope. A registrant’s actual business model (CC Serve’s partnering with banks) does not immunize it from a finding that its registered services overlap with a bank’s offerings if the terms in the registration or application naturally encompass such overlap. For students, that underscores the importance of precise class descriptions and the risks of broad identifications in prosecution and opposition practice.

## Factor 6 vacated: improper narrowing to credit card services despite broader similarity finding

The sixth DuPont factor examines the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods and services. Evidence of third‑party registration and use is relevant under this factor because it can show a mark is relatively weak and entitled to narrower protection. The legal principle is that “a crowded field” of similar marks teaches consumers to distinguish among similar marks and diminishes each mark’s exclusive scope.

The Board in Apex considered third‑party evidence of ASPIRE‑formative marks. Apex submitted numerous exhibits showing third‑party uses of “Aspire” or Aspire‑formative marks across the financial services sector. But the Board—having earlier concluded the parties’ services were highly similar—narrowed its consideration under factor six to third‑party marks used for “credit card services” alone, excluding marks used for other banking and financial services. The Board concluded that only nine Aspire marks were relevant and that that number was not “considerable” enough to demonstrate weakness. The Federal Circuit held this narrowing to be an error.

The doctrinal point is critical: DuPont factor assessments must be consistent in how the relevant goods or services are defined. If the Board’s analysis of factor two finds that banking and credit‑card services overlap or are partially legally identical, the scope of “similar goods or services” for the sixth factor cannot be truncated to a narrower subset (credit card services only) without justification. Precedent the court cited—Olde Tyme Foods and Juice Generation—makes plain that third‑party marks need not be on literally identical goods; marks used on similar goods or services are probative. The Board’s inconsistent scope effectively insulated CC Serve’s mark from evidence that might demonstrate that “Aspire” is widely used across the broader financial-services field. Because the breadth of services considered under factor two was broader, the sixth factor should have considered third‑party marks used in that broader space.

Students should draw two lessons. One, the Board must define the “relevant” goods or services and then apply that definition consistently when comparing marks and when evaluating third‑party use. Two, when arguing factor six, practitioners should frame third‑party use evidence to match the full scope of services the Board has found comparable under factor two. On remand, the Board is required to reassess factor six using the broader, more realistic scope of similar services it already found, considering all Aspire‑formative marks used on banking and financing services that are similar to the parties’ services.

## Factor 1 vacated: commercial impression must be reassessed after strength is recalibrated

The first DuPont factor analyzes the marks in their entireties with respect to appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression. This is an inherently comparative and context‑sensitive inquiry: a mark’s potential to cause confusion depends not only on literal similarity but on how consumers perceive and mentally process the marks in the marketplace.

In Apex the Board initially evaluated the marks and concluded that the first factor weighed in favor of the registrant. But because the Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s factor six analysis, the court also required reconsideration of factor one. Why? Because third‑party use evidence can materially affect the commercial impression of a mark. If the Board, on remand, finds that ASPIRE is widely used across similar financial services and thus commercially weak, that finding could alter the weight the first factor gives to the marks’ similarities. A mark that is conceptually or commercially weak receives narrower protection; the same degree of literal similarity between marks may be less likely to support a finding of confusion when the registered mark is one among many similar marks in a crowded field.

This interdependence between DuPont factors is a practical point about trademark analysis: findings under one factor—particularly those about the nature of services and a mark’s strength—can and should inform the analysis under other factors, including the core mark‑to‑mark comparison. The Federal Circuit’s vacatur of the Board’s factor one finding reinforces that the Board must revisit mark similarity with fresh eyes after any recalibration of commercial strength under factor six.

For students, the teaching is methodological: when preparing or reviewing a likelihood‑of‑confusion case, think holistically. If you plan to introduce evidence that a mark is weak (through third‑party use, registrations, or marketplace descriptive use), explain how that evidence changes the commercial impression and thus the first factor. Conversely, if defending a mark, show that third-party uses are limited or materially distinguishable so that the mark retains a broad scope of protection.

## Standards of review applied: substantial evidence and legal error

Understanding how appellate courts review TTAB decisions is essential for litigating and predicting outcomes. The Federal Circuit applies two complementary standards. Findings of fact—such as the Board’s determinations about the scope of a party’s services, the number and nature of third‑party uses, and the circumstances of marketing—are reviewed for substantial evidence. Substantial evidence requires that a reasonable mind could accept the evidence as adequate to support the Board’s findings; it is not a de novo reweighing. Legal conclusions, including the application and construction of legal standards and the weighing of DuPont factors as questions of law grounded on subsidiary fact findings, are reviewed de novo.

In Apex, the court affirmed the Board’s factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar because substantial evidence supported that conclusion. But the court found legal error in how the Board applied that finding to its consideration of factor six (a legal error in scope and consistency), which warranted vacatur and remand. This mixed approach—defer to factual findings so long as supported by evidence, but correct legal misapplication—is typical and instructive: a successful appeal will often hinge on identifying not an absence of evidence but an inconsistency in legal analysis or the wrongful application of legal standards to the facts the Board did find.

Students should therefore scrutinize TTAB decisions for two types of reversible mistakes: (1) insufficient evidentiary support for a factual finding; and (2) legal errors in applying principled standards (for example, inconsistent definitions of the relevant goods or consumers across factors). The Apex decision exemplifies the second category—an application error—rather than mere lack of evidence.

## Remand instructions: reconsider scope of third-party marks and mark comparison

The Federal Circuit’s mandate to the Board was precise in scope and instructive as to how the Board must proceed on remand. The Board must:

- Reassess DuPont factor six by considering third‑party marks used on all services that the Board previously found to be similar to the parties’ services. Because the Board had already found Apex’s “banking and financing services” highly similar (and partially legally identical) to CC Serve’s “credit card services,” the universe of third‑party marks eligible for consideration should include Aspire and Aspire‑formative marks used for banking, financing, payment processing, and other related financial services—not only literal “credit card services.”

- Reevaluate the commercial strength of CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark, taking into account the extent, nature, and consumer recognition of third‑party uses in the now‑properly defined service space. The Board must distinguish conceptual strength (inherent distinctiveness) from commercial strength (market recognition), and weigh evidence that a mark is descriptive or commonly used by third parties.

- Reexamine DuPont factor one—similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression—in light of any altered finding about CC Serve’s mark strength. If ASPIRE is shown to be commercially weak, the Board must explain whether the apparently similar elements between ASPIRE and ASPIRE BANK are likely to confuse a typical consumer despite the crowded field.

- Maintain consistent definitions of the relevant goods, services, and consumers across all factors. Where the parties’ services are found to be similar or overlapping, the Board should apply that same scope when defining the consumer audience and when comparing marks or evaluating competing marketplace uses.

Practically, the Board’s reconsideration must be reasoned and make clear why particular third‑party uses are or are not probative. It must explain the relevance of each piece of evidence to a mark’s strength and to consumer perception. If the Board discounts categories of third‑party evidence, it should articulate the reasons: temporal remoteness, different channels of trade, different consumer sophistication, or clear material differences in the services offered under the third-party marks.

## Practical implications for financial services branding strategies

Apex provides several actionable lessons for brand owners and practitioners in the financial-services sector.

First, choose marks that are distinctive in both concept and marketplace practice. The word “Aspire” is a laudatory verb—a kind of aspirational term that may be conceptually weak for services where aspiration is a common marketing theme. Particularly in industries like finance, where motivational words are popular, applicants should anticipate third‑party use and the probable limitation of trademark scope.

Second, prosecutions should use precise identifications of services. Broad identifications like “banking and financing services” can encompass many activities and make an applicant vulnerable to oppositions where prior users have registrations covering particular niches (e.g., credit‑card program management). If you wish to avoid conflict with existing registrations, consider narrowing the application identification (within the constraints of the applicant’s intended use) or providing evidence distinguishing channels of trade, classes of customers, or methods of marketing.

Third, collect and present third‑party use evidence early and comprehensively. If you are defending against an opposition, demonstrate that the prior mark is used in a narrow niche or that third‑party uses are few and materially different. If you are attacking a registration, marshal evidence showing a crowded field—registrations, websites, advertising, and third‑party branding across the full suite of relevant services. In Apex the breadth and nature of third‑party Aspire uses across financial services were central. Presenting them bundled by service type, channels of trade, and contemporaneous marketplace recognition will be persuasive.

Fourth, account for business models that involve partnerships and co‑branding. CC Serve’s model—managing credit‑card programs while banks issue the consumer‑facing cards—illustrates that functional distinctions in corporate structure do not necessarily determine the DuPont analysis. When a program manager or payments processor operates under a mark in conjunction with a bank, that mark may be treated as a source-identifying mark in the same marketplace as a bank’s retail brands.

Fifth, consider consumer sophistication evidence and consumer surveys when appropriate. Financial services range in consumer sophistication from routine retail banking customers to sophisticated institutional clients. Evidence that the relevant consumers are pro‑sophisticated professionals may reduce the likelihood of confusion; evidence of low consumer attention or habitual behavior in online banking may support the opposite.

Finally, keep uses and registrations current: pre‑1998 registrations like CC Serve’s may retain strong priority, but marketplace realities and subsequent third‑party adoption can erode exclusivity. Trademark owners should monitor and, where necessary, police third‑party uses; potential challengers should document and present the market’s evolution.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful case for understanding how the TTAB’s DuPont analysis should be coherent and consistent across factors. The second DuPont factor’s finding of high similarity between banking and credit‑card services was supported by substantial evidence and affirmed. But the Federal Circuit correctly required the Board to apply that finding uniformly when assessing third‑party use evidence under factor six and to revisit the first factor once commercial strength is recalibrated. For students and practitioners, the decision highlights operational rules: define the marketplace carefully, present third‑party evidence that matches that definition, and recognize the interdependence of DuPont factors—especially how a finding about a mark’s strength ripples through the mark‑to‑mark comparison.

# Comparative Jurisprudence: Key Federal Circuit and TTAB Precedents

This chapter situates Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., 2025 WL ___ (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025), within the Federal Circuit’s developing body of law on Section 2(d) likelihood-of-confusion disputes. The Apex Bank decision is a useful focal point because it both applies established DuPont principles and articulates a practical consistency principle: when the Board finds services to be highly similar (or legally identical) under the second DuPont factor, that factual finding should inform the scope of inquiry under related factors—especially the sixth (crowded field/third-party marks) and the first (marks compared in their entireties to determine commercial impression). To understand Apex’s holding and its implications, we must review the precedent that frames DuPont analysis, the proper use of third‑party evidence, and the standards governing appellate review. The following sections examine the principal cases and explain how each doctrine operated in Apex and how law students and practitioners should apply the principles to new fact patterns.

## Coach Services: relatedness of goods/services and third-party registrations

Coach Services v. Triumph Learning, 668 F.3d 1356 (Fed. Cir. 2012), is often cited for the principle that the goods or services of the parties need not be identical to support a finding of likelihood of confusion; instead, the relevant inquiry is whether the goods or services are “related in some manner” or marketed in such a way that consumers could believe they come from the same source. Coach emphasizes that evidence of relatedness may come from the nature of the goods and services, their channels of trade, typical purchasers, or third‑party registrations that cover both categories of goods and services.

Apex relied on the Coach principle when the Board examined whether CC Serve’s “credit card services” and Apex’s “banking and financing services” were related. The Board concluded—based on dictionary definitions, the parties’ descriptions, and third‑party registrations—that the services were highly similar and even partially legally identical. That determination is squarely within the scope of Coach’s guidance: the Board did not have to find that a bank and a credit card processor are the same business in all respects, but only that the services are related and marketed in ways that could cause consumer confusion. Coach thus informs factor‑two analysis: examiners and the Board should look beyond mere labels to the real-world nature, channels, and partnerships that connect two services.

For students: when analyzing relatedness under DuPont factor two, assemble multiple streams of evidence—statutory or dictionary definitions, specimen or application descriptions, third‑party registrations that cover both services, websites or promotional materials showing overlap in channels or target consumers, and evidence of commercial relationships (e.g., banks issuing cards serviced by third‑party processors). Each item complements the others and may together satisfy the “related in some manner” standard.

## Palm Bay: probative value of third-party registrations and weakness

Palm Bay Imports, Inc. v. Veuve Clicquot Ponsardin Maison Fondee En 1772, 396 F.3d 1369 (Fed. Cir. 2005), addresses the probative value of third‑party registrations and use evidence in showing a mark’s weakness. Palm Bay explains that third‑party registrations and evidence of use can be admitted to show that a descriptive or common element is weak and entitled to a narrow scope of protection. The key point is that such evidence tends to show that the marketplace is saturated with marks using the same or similar formative elements, and thus consumers have become accustomed to distinguishing among such marks.

In Apex, the parties disputed whether CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark was commercially or conceptually weak because other market participants used “Aspire” or Aspire‑formative marks in the financial services space. The Board, citing Palm Bay, acknowledged third‑party uses but concluded the number of similar marks was not “considerable” or “ubiquitous” enough to demonstrate weakness. Palm Bay teaches that the strength or weakness inquiry is fact intensive: the Board must evaluate the quality, quantity, and nature of the third‑party evidence and explain how that evidence undermines (or does not undermine) a mark’s distinctiveness.

For students: third‑party registrations alone have limited probative value; the Board and courts prefer evidence of marketplace use—advertisements, screenshots, specimens—because such evidence shows actual consumer exposure. The weight assigned to that evidence depends on its volume relative to the field, its recency, and whether the marks are used on services that the relevant consumer would actually encounter when making purchasing decisions.

## Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin: crowded field evidence and its effect on scope

Juice Generation, Inc. v. GS Enters. LLC, 794 F.3d 1334 (Fed. Cir. 2015), and Jack Wolfskin Ausrustung Fur Draussen GmbH & Co. KGAA v. New Millennium Sports, S.L.U., 797 F.3d 1363 (Fed. Cir. 2015), both illustrate how “crowded field” evidence affects the scope of protection afforded to a mark. Juice Generation found that 26 third‑party registrations and uses constituted “a considerable number” that was probative of weakness for a short, common word element; Jack Wolfskin stressed the importance of voluminous and representative examples showing that the public has been trained to distinguish among similar marks (the opinion pointed to 14 notable examples and many additional registrations and sales figures).

These decisions show two related points. First, the number of third‑party marks is important—what constitutes “considerable” depends on the marketplace and how often consumers are likely to see such marks. Second, courts expect context and explanation: raw lists of registrations are less persuasive than carefully documented, industry‑relevant examples showing consumer exposure, similarity of services, or even geographic breadth. Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin also emphasize that a crowded field can narrow a mark’s protection; where the field is crowded, the owner’s monopoly over a formative element should be limited to prevent unfairly blocking newcomers from using otherwise weak but descriptive elements.

Apex illustrates the interplay between factor two and factor six: the Board found the services highly similar but then limited its sixth‑factor inquiry to marks used only for “credit card services,” excluding Aspire marks used in other banking contexts. The Federal Circuit vacated that narrowing. Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin guide the remand: if, as the Board found, banking and credit‑card services are highly similar, then the Board must consider the broader universe of Aspire‑formative marks used across banking and finance when assessing whether the field is crowded and how broad CC Serve’s protection should be.

For students and practitioners: when assembling crowded‑field evidence, present both breadth (how many similar marks are in use) and depth (how similar those marks are in channels and consumers and how much exposure they have earned). Show not only registrations but specimens, market share or sales data, licensing, and consumer testimony where possible. If you have evidence of consumer sophistication or distinguishing house marks, include that too, because a crowded field interacts with purchaser sophistication in reducing likely confusion.

## Olde Tyme Foods: ‘similar’ does not mean ‘identical’ under the factors

Olde Tyme Foods, Inc. v. Roundy’s, Inc., 961 F.2d 200 (Fed. Cir. 1992), stands for the simple but important proposition that “similar” does not require “identical.” The Olde Tyme court clarified that DuPont’s factor concerning the number and nature of similar marks (and related inquiries) does not demand exact identity of goods or services; rather, it requires a finding that the goods are similar enough that consumers would be likely to associate them with a single source.

Apex draws on Olde Tyme to support the proposition that the sixth DuPont factor may—and often should—look beyond literally identical goods to those that are similar in the sense recognized under factor two. The Federal Circuit in Apex explicitly invoked Olde Tyme and related cases to explain why the Board erred in narrowing third‑party consideration to only credit card services after it had already found credit card and banking services to be highly similar. Olde Tyme therefore functions as a cross‑factor coherence rule: once the Board finds similarity for relatedness, it must not impose a stricter similarity requirement for examining the marketplace for similar marks.

For application: when litigating, resist attempts by the opposing party to compartmentalize evidence by calling it “not identical” on a narrow, literal basis. Show how the goods or services are used in the same channels, targeted to the same consumers, or otherwise overlap so that the court or Board may rely on Olde Tyme to broaden the inquiry appropriately.

## M2 Software and Bose: articulation of standards of review and factor-by-factor findings

M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Commc’ns, Inc., 450 F.3d 1378 (Fed. Cir. 2006), and Bose Corp. v. QSC Audio Prods., Inc., 293 F.3d 1367 (Fed. Cir. 2002), address appellate standards and the necessary factual rigor in DuPont analyses. M2 Software reiterates that legal questions are reviewed de novo while factual findings are reviewed for substantial evidence. Bose adds that each DuPont factor presents a factual question and that the Board’s analysis should make clear findings on which courts can perform a substantial‑evidence review.

The practical lesson from these cases appears in Apex’s opinion: the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s factual finding on factor two under substantial‑evidence review, but it vacated and remanded factors one and six because the Board’s legal framing and selective narrowing of evidence were inconsistent with its earlier factual findings. The appellate court’s error correction reflects Bose and M2 Software: an appellate court can and will require the Board to explain its reasoning, apply consistent scopes, and ensure that legal framework and factual findings are aligned.

For students: when evaluating Board decisions, identify whether the Board has (1) made explicit factual findings for the DuPont factors it considered, (2) tied those findings to evidence in the record, and (3) applied a consistent legal standard across factors. If the Board changes scope or relies on a narrower standard in a later factor without explanation—especially after making a broad factual finding—the decision is vulnerable on appeal under the M2/Bose framework.

## I.AM.Symbolic: commercial impression and mark comparison in their entireties

In re I.AM.Symbolic, LLC, 866 F.3d 1315 (Fed. Cir. 2017), the Federal Circuit emphasized that the first DuPont factor focuses on the marks “in their entireties,” considering appearance, sound, connotation, and overall commercial impression. I.AM.Symbolic reiterates that an analysis which cherry‑picks elements of a mark or mechanically compares components without considering the overall impression is insufficient. The court also reminded tribunals to consider how consumers encounter marks in real life—how they are displayed, pronounced, and perceived.

Apex’s remand of the first factor derives directly from I.AM.Symbolic’s teachings. Because the Board’s evaluation of commercial impression might change if the sixth factor is reweighed to include a broader set of Aspire uses (thereby possibly showing weakness of the formative element), the Board must reconsider whether the ASPIRE BANK word and design marks would create a similar commercial impression to CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark. I.AM.Symbolic requires the Board to perform that comparison holistically and to explain how any changes in perceived strength or narrowing of protection affect the overall likelihood‑of‑confusion calculus.

For classroom application: when comparing marks under the first DuPont factor, avoid analyzing only dominant or distinctive elements in isolation. Instead, simulate consumer perception—how is the mark used in context? Are there distinctive design elements or additional words that change the commercial impression? Is the sound prominent, or does the visual design dominate? Remember that differences in trade channels and marketing-focused matter in forming consumer impressions.

## Harmonizing these cases with Apex Bank’s consistency principle

Apex Bank contributes a clarifying principle of practical importance: factual determinations about similarity made in one DuPont factor should constrain (and inform) the scope of inquiry under other related factors. The court held that after the Board found the parties’ services to be highly similar (and in part legally identical) under factor two, it erred by later constricting the sixth‑factor comparison to only marks used for “credit card services” while excluding marks used in the broader banking and financial services arena. The Federal Circuit’s ruling harmonizes earlier precedent in several ways.

First, it operationalizes Olde Tyme and Coach: similarity is not a binary, narrow concept; once a finding of high similarity is made, the Board should not adopt a stricter similarity standard for other factors without justification. Second, it follows Palm Bay, Juice Generation, and Jack Wolfskin by directing the Board to consider the broader marketplace when assessing field‑crowding and mark weakness—particularly those third‑party marks consumers would realistically encounter when deciding between services that the Board itself has found related. Third, consistent with M2 Software and Bose, Apex requires the Board to articulate and reconcile its factual findings across factors so that an appellate court can review the record meaningfully.

This “consistency principle” does not mean the Board must treat every related factor identically; rather, it must apply coherent boundaries of similarity. If factor two identifies the relevant goods or services as covering, for example, banking and credit‑card services broadly, then third‑party evidence under factor six should encompass the same or reasonably overlapping categories, and the Board must explain any deviation. Similarly, commercial impression analysis under factor one must consider whether market saturation or weakness of a formative element meaningfully alters how consumers perceive source—a determination that cannot be made in isolation from the sixth factor’s findings.

Practical implications for practitioners and examiners:

- When you obtain a favorable factual finding on relatedness (factor two), press the Board or tribunal to use that same scope when evaluating third‑party evidence and commercial impression. Conversely, if you seek to limit the scope under later factors, you must provide a reasoned basis for narrowing the relevant universe of marks or services.
- Assemble third‑party evidence that matches the scope you want the Board to acknowledge. If your theory depends on a broad marketplace, include registrations and use evidence across the relevant related services—not just identical labels.
- Anticipate the Board’s obligation, highlighted by M2/Bose, to explain how each DuPont factor was considered and to tie findings to record evidence. If the explanation is missing or inconsistent, the decision is vulnerable on appeal.

Conclusion

Apex Bank reaffirms and refines key strands of DuPont jurisprudence. It confirms that the Board’s findings of relatedness under factor two should inform the universe of comparison for factor six (crowded field/third‑party marks) and that changes in perceived mark strength feed back into the holistic commercial‑impression inquiry of factor one. Reading Apex alongside Coach, Palm Bay, Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin, Olde Tyme Foods, M2 Software, Bose, and I.AM.Symbolic yields a coherent framework: the Board must (1) evaluate relatedness with real‑world evidence, (2) treat third‑party use as contextually probative of weakness and scope, (3) compare marks in their entireties with attention to commercial impression, and (4) make clear, consistent factual findings that a court can review for substantial evidence. For law students and practitioners, the controlling strategy is to marshal evidence that aligns the scopes of these inquiries—so that once the Board accepts a broad or narrow definition of the marketplace, it must either apply that definition across relevant DuPont factors or explain why it does not.

# Channels of Trade, Consumer Sophistication, and Conditions of Purchase

This chapter examines three interrelated strands of the likelihood-of-confusion inquiry that often decide trademark disputes in the financial-services sector: the channels of trade and the presumptions that attach to broad identifications; the role of consumer sophistication and the conditions of purchase (the care with which consumers make selecting decisions); and how online delivery, price, and a crowded marketplace interact with mark strength and similarity. The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., decided in 2025, provides a concrete vehicle for showing how these doctrines operate in practice and how errors in applying them can require remand. Read together, these themes demonstrate that seemingly small choices about how parties frame their services and how the Board defines the relevant consumer and channels of trade can have outsized effects in financial-services cases.

## Presumptions for unrestricted identifications: all normal channels and classes of consumers

When an applicant or registrant describes its goods or services broadly—without limiting channels of trade, classes of purchasers, or types of marketing—the Board and courts apply a familiar evidentiary presumption: the identified goods or services encompass all of the normal channels of trade and all classes of consumers for those goods or services. That presumption is practical: a trademark owner who chooses a broad identification cannot later argue that its mark is limited to a narrow market when asserting a likelihood of confusion. Conversely, an applicant defending against an opposition cannot rely on an examiner’s or registrant’s broad identification to narrow the field unless the record contains evidence to justify a narrower scope.

In Apex Bank, the Board made a key factual finding—based on the parties’ identifications and meanings of the services—that Apex’s banking and financing services and CC Serve’s credit card services were “highly similar,” and in part “legally identical.” That finding carried consequences. The Federal Circuit relied on it to reverse the Board’s narrow consideration of third-party marks under the sixth DuPont factor. The Board had limited its inquiry to marks used for credit card services, excluding marks that used “Aspire” in connection with other banking and financial services. The court faulted that approach: if the parties’ services are found to be highly similar (or overlap), the universe of comparable third-party marks must be at least as broad as the scope used to determine similarity. In short, you cannot find services overlapping for one DuPont factor and simultaneously treat them as narrower for another factor without reasoned explanation.

Practically, the presumption of all-normal-channels means a party should:

- Pay attention to the pleaded identifications. If defending an application, consider proposing reasonable limitations to channels or classes of purchasers early in prosecution or show evidence that the marketplace is narrower. If pursuing an opposition, exploit the breadth when arguing overlap of channels.
- Anticipate that a finding of similarity for the second DuPont factor will broaden the universe of third-party uses and channels relevant to other factors (for example, the sixth factor on third-party marks and the consideration of channels of trade under the overall likelihood analysis). Apex teaches that the Board must retain a consistent scope unless record evidence supports a narrower focus.
- Introduce concrete record evidence (e.g., marketing materials, distribution agreements, third-party registrations that themselves include explicit channel limitations) if you want the Board to treat a service as reaching only a subset of the usual channels or consumers.

## Consumer sophistication in banking and credit contexts: when it helps and when it does not

Consumer sophistication is one of the recurrent contextual considerations in a DuPont analysis. The basic intuition is straightforward: a more sophisticated or discriminating purchaser is less likely to be confused by similar marks. But sophistication is neither a talisman nor a one-size-fits-all defense. Courts look to actual marketplace realities, not labels. The critical questions are: who is the relevant purchaser? How complex or expensive is the transaction? How much time and attention does a typical purchaser expend when choosing between the services at issue?

In financial services, “sophistication” can mean different things depending on the product and relationship. Institutional purchasers selecting a commercial banking partner, or a corporation negotiating a private-label credit-card program, typically exercise high sophistication — they conduct due diligence, compare competitors, and negotiate lengthy contracts. On the other hand, an ordinary consumer choosing a retail bank or a consumer credit card often makes a quicker, lower-effort choice influenced by convenience, advertising, or a branch location. Even within consumer markets there are gradations: someone opening a mortgage may be more deliberate than someone signing an instant online deposit account.

Apex illustrates how the Board framed the relevant class of consumers. Because it concluded that the parties’ services were highly similar and overlapped in the credit-card/financial-services space, the Board limited its view of the “relevant public” to consumers of credit card services. That narrowed the Board’s focus for the sixth DuPont factor (and, in turn, its inquiry into mark weakness). On appeal the Federal Circuit faulted the Board’s subsequent narrowing of third-party comparisons—holding that the Board could not narrow the consumer universe for one factor after finding broad overlap in another. Apex thus underscores two lessons:

- Precisely identifying the relevant consumer class is essential. A party that convinces the Board that its services are narrowly targeted may obtain the benefit of a sophisticated-purchaser analysis for some factors; conversely, an opponent that secures a broad overlap finding may force a broader consumer lens.
- Evidence of sophistication must be specific and tied to the product at issue. General assertions that “bank customers are sophisticated” will not be enough. Courts want evidence showing how consumers make the particular purchasing decision: typical search and selection behavior, use of brokers or intermediaries, necessity of contractual review, or presence of custom underwriting. Affidavits, industry surveys, consumer-decision studies, and example agreements can all show higher sophistication.

When sophistication helps: where the record shows that the relevant purchasers are institutions, businesses, or consumers engaged in careful comparative shopping (for example, negotiated business banking relationships, syndicated loans, or large commercial lending arrangements), courts will often give weight to the “conditions of purchase” and find a reduced likelihood of confusion.

When sophistication does not help: for common consumer financial products that are relatively low-commitment or purchased impulsively (e.g., quick consumer checking accounts, standard credit cards marketed broadly), courts frequently treat purchasers as less careful. The mere complexity of financial services as a sector does not automatically mean purchasers are sophisticated with respect to brand source.

## Price and care: credit products versus deposit accounts

Price and the resulting degree of consumer care are closely connected. Higher-priced goods or services generally invite more careful consideration, while lower-priced or routine transactions tend to be made quickly and with less brand scrutiny. In financial services, however, price is a complex metric: some high-dollar products (mortgages, commercial loans) involve careful shopping; other significant-value products (credit-card accounts that carry large revolving balances) can be obtained with minimal friction via online sign-up or in-branch offers. Similarly, deposit accounts—while often of modest initiation cost—may have long-term implications for consumers’ relationship with a bank, suggesting potential for more careful selection in some contexts.

Applying these ideas: when a party argues that consumers will exercise heightened care, they should show that the purchase involves substantial monies, binding contractual commitments, time-consuming underwriting, or negotiation. For example:

- Mortgages and commercial credit facilities: high stakes, multi-stage underwriting, counsel involved — these tend to support an inference of more careful purchasing and may reduce the chance of confusion.
- Credit cards and consumer checking accounts: despite potential long-term impact, initial acquisition is often low-friction (online apps, point-of-sale offers). Many consumers accept cards or open accounts quickly, which diminishes the protective value of “sophistication” arguments.

In Apex, the Board recognized the variety of services grouped under “credit card services” (issuing cards, processing, authorization, account management). The Board explicitly described the credit card services domain as including both account management and issuance—services that consumers may experience in different ways and with different levels of attention. This points to a practical approach: break “financial services” into the discrete transactions that consumers actually perform and tie evidence of care (or lack of it) to the discrete transaction. An opposition should not rely on a generic statement that “banking customers are careful” but instead show the actual purchasing steps and the salience of brand at each step.

## Online versus brick-and-mortar delivery of financial services

The delivery channel matters. The proliferation of digital banking and fintech platforms has changed how consumers discover, compare, and select financial services. Courts increasingly recognize that online channels can amplify some kinds of confusion (similar logos appearing in search results or app stores) while attenuating others (clear web pages and disclosures that make source distinctions obvious). Whether online delivery increases or decreases the likelihood of confusion depends on how consumers encounter marks in practice.

Key dynamics to consider:

- Visibility and juxtaposition: online marketplaces, app stores, and search results often present multiple providers in proximity. A consumer seeing two “Aspire” marks in a single search results page may be more prone to confusion than a consumer visiting a single branch location where in-person signage and attendant conversation provide multiple cues.
- Control and richness of presentation: registrants and applicants can control their own web pages and app listings to emphasize distinctive design elements, disclaimers, and corporate identity. High-quality brand presentation can reduce confusion when consumers land on a company’s dedicated property.
- Speed of selection: on the web, consumers often click through quickly, increasing the risk of confusion where marks are similar and the browsing experience does not immediately make source differences obvious.
- Cross-channel exposure: many financial-services consumers interact with firms both online and offline. Where consumers begin their relationship online but later complete transactions in branch (or vice versa), brand confusion can propagate across channels.

For litigants, practical rules of thumb follow: gather evidence about how consumers typically encounter and select the relevant service. This can include analytics demonstrating pathways to account opening, shopper-experience studies, screenshots of search results and app-store presentations, and affidavits from customers describing their experience. In Apex, although the opinion did not focus on online versus brick-and-mortar as a dispositive point, the Board’s initial narrowing to credit-card consumers implicitly recognized the concrete ways consumers encounter credit-card products (often via partnerships between banks and processors and via online offers). A properly developed record showing the predominant channels for acquisition—online sign-up pages, in-branch applications, co-branded card offers at merchants—would have helped the Board and the court define the right universe of channels and consumers.

## Interplay with mark strength and similarity: sophisticated consumers in crowded fields

The sixth DuPont factor—evidence of third-party use of similar marks on similar goods—operates at the intersection of channels of trade and consumer sophistication. Evidence of widespread third-party use of a word or device in a product category tends to show the mark is conceptually or commercially weak and thus entitled to a more limited scope of protection. The rationale is that when many competitors use similar marks, consumers are trained to attend to subtle differences, not to assume common source.

Apex exposes how calibrating the scope of comparison influences the assessment of mark strength. The Board, after finding the parties’ services highly similar, nevertheless limited its sixth-factor analysis to third-party marks used only for credit card services; it excluded marks in other banking and financing contexts. The Federal Circuit held that limitation inconsistent: once the Board determined that banking and credit-card services were highly similar (and thus within the same relevant market), it had to treat third-party marks across the broader banking and finance spectrum as relevant evidence of crowdedness. Put differently, the field against which you measure “crowdedness” must match the field in which you assert similarity.

Sophistication modifies this relationship. In a crowded field of consumer-facing, low-commitment products with unsophisticated purchasers, even a sizable number of third-party uses may not inoculate a mark from protection: consumers are less likely to devote effort distinguishing numerous similar marks if they make quick choices. Conversely, in markets where purchasers are sophisticated and make careful comparisons between suppliers, evidence of crowdedness can be more powerful in narrowing the scope of protection.

Thus, consider the following when arguing mark strength or weakness:

- Define the competitive field carefully and consistently. If you argue that your services overlap with a broad banking field, you cannot ignore evidence of third-party “Aspire” uses in wider banking contexts.
- Link consumer sophistication evidence to the crowdedness argument. Demonstrate that purchasers operating in the crowded field are the same purchasers whose restraint or discriminating behavior matters.
- Produce market evidence that shows real-world third-party co-existence without confusion: co-branded marketing, contemporaneous adverts showing multiple “Aspire” marks in use in the same channels, and consumer awareness studies can all be persuasive.

In Apex, the court vacated the Board’s sixth-factor ruling because the Board’s narrower definition artificially minimized the impression of a crowded field and thus might have overallocated protective scope to CC Serve’s mark. That decision highlights the importance of consistent analytical scope across DuPont factors.

## Developing evidence for or against heightened care in financial decision-making

Because the DuPont analysis and the inquiry into consumer sophistication are so fact-sensitive, litigants and counsel should plan tailored evidentiary programs. The following categories of evidence are particularly relevant in financial-services disputes:

1. Transactional and procedural evidence
   - Typical account opening flows (online and in-branch): screenshots, step-by-step guides, click-through paths.
   - Documentation requirements and underwriting processes: required documentation lists, time-to-approval metrics, and whether underwriting is automated or manual.
   - Length and nature of contracts and termination rights.

2. Pricing and economic indicators
   - Fee schedules, APRs, initiation fees, and balances to show how economically significant the purchase is.
   - Comparative tables showing differences in what consumers pay or get under the two services.

3. Marketing and channels of distribution
   - Advertising materials, co-branding agreements (for example, bank-processor contracts for credit cards), and placement evidence (search results, app-store screens, merchant point-of-sale visuals).
   - Distribution agreements indicating whether services are sold primarily through third parties, brokers, a bank’s branch network, or direct-to-consumer online channels.

4. Consumer behavior and surveys
   - Trade or independent consumer surveys tailored to the precise question of source confusion (with careful survey design and expert testimony).
   - Analytics and consumer journey studies demonstrating how consumers discover and choose services.

5. Industry practices and testimony
   - Declarations from industry participants explaining how products are selected, who makes the decision, and how brand identity functions in vendor selection.
   - Evidence of standard market practices (e.g., whether customers typically rely on price, rewards, or brand for card selection).

6. Evidence of marketplace coexistence and actual confusion
   - Instances of actual confusion (complaints, misdirected calls, customer communications) can be powerful, though their absence is not dispositive.
   - Evidence of long-term, peaceful coexistence in the same channels can support arguments of low likelihood of confusion.

When planning discovery and building a record, parties should be mindful that general industry evidence has less probative force than evidence tied to the actual product and consumer decision point at issue. Apex shows that once a tribunal determines services are substantially similar, the breadth of the record of third-party uses and channels becomes highly material to both the sixth and first DuPont factors.

## Closing practical guidance

- Be consistent about market definition. If you win a finding that services overlap broadly, expect your opponent to insist that third-party evidence be drawn from the same broad universe.
- Develop evidence about how consumers encounter and choose the specific service at issue—pricing, process, channels, and marketing—rather than relying on general statements about banking customers.
- Use surveys and user-experience analytics to demonstrate (or refute) sophistication and conditions of purchase; ensure survey design is tailored and defensible.
- For applicants, consider narrowing identifications during prosecution if you can show the real-world market is more specific than the broad class asserted in your application. For opposers, leverage broad identifications to capture third-party evidence showing crowdedness.
- Remember that online presentation and cross-channel shopping are increasingly central in financial services: document how the marks appear in search, app-stores, and co-brand offers, and evaluate how those presentations affect the salience of mark differences.

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. illustrates the cost of inconsistent analysis: a finding that services are highly similar requires a commensurate, broad approach to factors that measure crowdedness, commercial weakness, and the marks’ overall commercial impression. Thoughtful, targeted evidence about channels, purchaser care, and the actual conditions of purchase will often determine which way that analysis tips.

# Designing Identifications and Branding Strategies in Financial Services

This chapter translates doctrinal rules developed in trademark opposition practice—most recently illustrated by Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., 2025 WL ___ (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025)—into practical strategy for practitioners and brand owners operating in the crowded, overlapping world of financial services. Apex Bank is an instructive decision: the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that credit-card services and “banking and financing services” are highly similar, but it vacated the Board’s narrower treatment of third‑party uses and the first‑factor analysis because the Board failed to carry the scope of similarity it found into its DuPont six (third‑party use) and DuPont one (mark similarity/commercial impression) analyses. That error provides a concrete lesson: the scope you establish under one DuPont factor shapes what you may properly rely on under others. Below we explain the doctrinal rules and give practical drafting, searching, clearance, and evidence-building strategies tailored to banks, card issuers, processors, fintechs, and their counsel.

Overview of the legal framework and the lesson of Apex Bank
- A Section 2(d) opposition asks whether confusion is likely, a legal determination based on underlying factual findings under the DuPont factors. The Board need only analyze the DuPont factors that are relevant and of record. But its findings must be coherent across factors: if the Board finds services highly similar under factor two, it cannot limit the universe of third‑party marks for factor six to a narrower definition of similarity inconsistent with that finding. That was the Federal Circuit’s precise criticism in Apex Bank.
- Practically, the holding means that a party’s description of services (the identification of goods/services) has cascading consequences. The breadth of the identification affects (1) whether the services are treated as overlapping or identical, (2) which third‑party uses are “similar” and therefore probative under DuPont six, and (3) how the commercial impression analysis under DuPont one is performed.

The rest of the chapter unpacks how to draft identifications, how to think about modifiers like “Bank,” how to search and clear marks across adjacent categories, how to build evidence of strength or weakness, how to use third‑party use to narrow an opponent’s scope of protection, and how to mitigate exposure through coexistence, consent agreements, and limitation amendments.

## Drafting identifications: breadth versus specificity tradeoffs

What an identification says matters. Under the Lanham Act and TTAB practice, an identification of services sets the universe of goods/services that the registration presumptively covers. That universe governs (1) the substantive question of whether the services overlap (DuPont two), (2) what third‑party marks should be considered similar for purposes of DuPont six, and (3) the channels and consumers relevant to the commercial impression analysis.

Two competing drafting philosophies arise.

- Broad identifications: Broad, omnibus language—“banking and financial services,” or “financial services;” “financial services, namely, consumer and commercial banking services”—maximizes coverage and future flexibility. The upside is obvious: you can expand your offerings without filing new applications. The downside is also direct: broad identifications invite oppositions and attacks based on a wide set of prior uses and third‑party registrations. A broad identification increases the chance the Board/TTAB (and a court) will find overlap with a prior user and open the door to third‑party evidence across many subcategories.

- Narrow, specific identifications: Precise itemization—“retail branch banking services; mobile banking services via a website; personal checking and savings accounts; consumer home mortgage lending”—limits registration coverage to those stated services. The upside is defensibility: narrower identifications reduce the universe of prior uses that can be shoehorned into an overlap finding. The downside is loss of flexibility; if the registrant later expands into different services (for example, card issuance when the initial registration excluded it), a new application will be required.

How to choose between breadth and specificity
- Consider business plan and timing. If the client intends to expand quickly into adjacent services, a broader application may be appealing—but only if the risk of conflict is low. If the market is crowded (many third‑party uses of the proposed house mark across banking, cards, payments, and fintech), narrow specificity can be a defensive tactic to avoid oppositions and an adverse DuPont six analysis.
- Think of overlapping marks and enforcement needs. If the brand will be used as a house mark across many products, breadth may be necessary for consistent branding. If the mark’s strength will be a litigation or licensing asset, broad scope may be desirable despite higher clearance costs.
- Use conditional drafting where useful. For intent‑to‑use filings, tailor the identification to the services you genuinely intend to launch in the near term; you can file follow‑on applications later (or amend to broaden, though that involves risk). For live use filings, your specimen limits the registration to the actual services in use, which helps if those services are specific.

Examples and practical phrasing
- Broad: “Banking and financial services; namely, retail banking services, commercial banking services, mortgage lending, and online banking services.”
- Narrow: “Retail branch banking services; provision of personal checking and savings accounts; online access to account information for deposit accounts.” (Avoid including “credit card services” if the business will not offer cards.)
- Hybrid: “Banking and financial services, namely, retail banking and online banking services; excluding credit card issuance and processing services.” (Explicit exclusion clauses can help create safe passage against existing card-issuer marks.)

Draft identifications with the DuPont consequences in mind: if your ID renders your services legally identical or highly similar to a prior user’s services, you should expect the Board to consider third‑party marks across the entire scope established by that identification.

## House marks and descriptive modifiers (e.g., ‘Bank’) and their effect on distinctiveness

Two common features of financial-service marks are house marks and descriptors like “Bank,” “Card,” “Payments,” or “Finance.” Both affect how the mark will be treated on distinctiveness grounds and the scope of protection a registrant can expect.

House marks
- A house mark is a brand that serves as an umbrella for multiple services (e.g., “ASPIRE” used on credit cards, checking accounts, loans). House marks can be powerful: they link many offerings to a single source and build cross‑product brand recognition. The legal effect is that house marks may be entitled to broader protection if their use across many products has created strong consumer association with a single source (secondary meaning at common law or well‑developed acquired distinctiveness if descriptive).
- But house marks often lead to broader DuPont analyses. If a house mark is used across cards and banking, courts and the TTAB will consider a wide field of third‑party uses under DuPont six. That can help or hurt: broad, consistent use can show strength; broad use across many competitors can show weakness.

Descriptive modifiers (e.g., “Bank”)
- Words like “bank,” “card,” “payments,” or “finance” are often descriptive or generic for certain services. Their presence typically reduces the mark’s conceptual distinctiveness and narrows the scope of protection for the composite mark. For example, “ASPIRE BANK” is composed of a presumably more distinctive term (“ASPIRE”) and a descriptive term (“BANK”). The TTAB and courts treat these composites under the likelihood‑of‑confusion test by considering the mark in its entirety but recognizing that descriptive elements lessen overall distinctiveness.
- Disclaimers are common: applicants will frequently disclaim exclusive rights to the generic/descriptive component (e.g., disclaiming “Bank” apart from the mark as shown). A disclaimer does not prevent the Board from finding likelihood of confusion—it only prevents the registrant from monopolizing the disclaimed term alone—but it is relevant to the commercial impression and how the mark is compared to others.

Practical advice regarding house marks and modifiers
- If you adopt a house mark plus descriptive modifier, emphasize the distinctive component in stylization, logo design, and use‑in‑commerce specimens. Logos and stylized elements can contribute to distinctiveness and registration prospects, but remember that a design mark protects only the exact stylization shown in the application; to maximize protection often combine a standard character filing for the word element with a design mark for the stylized treatment.
- Expect that a composite with a descriptive modifier will receive a narrower scope of protection: in DuPont one, the disclaimed or descriptive matter will be given less weight in the similarity analysis; the distinctive element will take center stage. In Apex Bank, “ASPIRE” is the contested element, and “BANK” is likely treated as descriptive by the tribunal. If “ASPIRE” is weak in the field, that weakness will carry into the “ASPIRE BANK” composite.
- Use disclaimers strategically. A disclaimer may be required; if so, accept it but counterbalance with strong evidence of acquired distinctiveness for the distinctive element or argue that the composite as a whole has acquired secondary meaning.

## Clearing financial services marks: searching across adjacent categories (cards, loans, fintech)

A proper clearance search in financial services must be broader than the narrow subcategory in which the client presently operates. The Apex Bank decision underscores why: services that appear distinct (credit-card servicing vs. retail bank accounts) can be treated as highly similar for DuPont purposes. Clearance must therefore map real marketplace relationships.

What to search
- Registrations and live (common‑law) uses for the identical or similar word(s). Search the USPTO TESS database for: identical marks; phonetically similar marks; and marks with the same or similar dominant word elements.
- Adjacent service categories: not only “banking services” but “credit card services,” “card processing,” “payment processing,” “card issuing,” “card program management,” “financial account servicing,” “fintech platforms,” “merchant services,” and “prepaid card services.” Because many firms partner (banks, processors, fintechs), evaluate “program manager” and “issuer processor” descriptions.
- Non‑financial overlapping channels: fintech companies often blur the line between “payment solutions” and “banking services,” and card issuers may also offer online account access and lending products. Google and web searches beyond TESS—press releases, app stores, partnership announcements—are critical.
- International uses and filings. Many financial brands are global; prior use outside the U.S. can be persuasive to the USPTO (and critical for global clearance strategy).
- Third‑party common law: domain names, app stores, social media handles, and press mentions. For cards and payment platforms, look for white‑label partnerships and “powered by” language.

Search methodology and scope
- Use keyword permutations and wildcard searching for word stems and common modifiers: e.g., "Aspire," "ASPR," "ASPI*", combined with "bank," "card," "card services," "payments," "issuer," "processor," "fintech," etc.
- Probe corporate filings and partnership announcements: many card program managers operate through banks and processors; a party claiming that it “does not offer bank services” may nonetheless be the public face of a product in partnership with a bank.
- Look for filings and uses that show the mark used across different services under a single brand—these uses can be especially damaging for a new entrant.

Interpreting search results in light of DuPont
- If your searches show many third‑party uses of a core word across payment, banking, and fintech categories, those results can be marshaled in DuPont six to argue that the mark is weak and entitled to a narrow scope of protection. Apex Bank shows that a tribunal must consider the proper scope of “similar services” when counting such third‑party uses.
- Conversely, if the field is not crowded—few uses across relevant categories—argue for broader protection and show that consumers will associate the mark with a single source.

Practical checklist for clearance
1. Run USPTO TESS searches—including live and dead registrations—and capture mark strings and identifications.
2. Run a comprehensive internet search for use (press releases, app listings, merchant materials).
3. Map partnerships and white‑label relationships; many payment brands operate through banks or processors.
4. Search domain names, social media handles, and app stores.
5. Evaluate whether proposed identification should be broad or narrow based on the density of potentially confusing marks.
6. Prepare a clearance memo describing risk and recommended identification scope.

## Building a record of strength: surveys, market share, advertising, media coverage

When a mark faces opposition, registrant strength is a critical battleground. Strength has two main aspects: conceptual strength (how distinctive is the mark on its face) and commercial strength (how well consumers actually recognize the mark as indicating source). Evidence of commercial strength can shift the DuPont analysis in multiple ways: it increases the likelihood that consumers will confuse marks, and it supports entitlement to a broader scope of protection.

Types of evidence and how to use them
- Sales figures and market share. Present clear, contemporaneous figures showing sales attributable to the mark and market share in the defined relevant market. Explain how the market is measured (e.g., “online-only consumer deposit accounts in the Southeastern U.S.”). The specificity of the market definition is crucial: general national figures are less informative if the relevant market is more narrow.
- Advertising expenditures and media coverage. Demonstrate the scale and reach of promotion: advertising spend, impressions, television/radio placement, digital ad metrics, social media followers, and PR coverage. Attach representative advertising specimens (print ads, web pages, app screenshots).
- Actual consumer recognition evidence. This includes consumer surveys, unsolicited media mentions, awards, and third‑party endorsements. Surveys must be designed legally and methodologically sound to be persuasive at the TTAB and on appeal.
- Length and breadth of use. Show continuous and uncontested use of the mark, and, where relevant, use across many services which may suggest house‑mark recognition.

Surveys—best practices and pitfalls
- Timing and universe. Surveys must focus on the proper universe of consumers (the “relevant market” as defined under DuPont two). In Apex Bank, the Board’s narrowing of consumers to “credit card services” shows why bad survey framing can mislead a tribunal. If your services are similar to many others, the survey population should reflect that.
- Proper controls and stimuli. Use proper side‑by‑side comparisons (both sight and sound where appropriate), control versus test groups, and realistic contexts (in‑person or online stimuli mirroring real decision situations). Avoid forced‑choice designs that may overstate confusion.
- Methodological documentation. Include the questionnaire, screening criteria, sampling method, sample size, and statistical analysis in the record. The TTAB and courts will scrutinize methodology carefully.
- Use of survey evidence at TTAB. Some TTAB panels give heavy weight to a well‑designed survey; others are skeptical. Consider whether a survey is cost‑effective versus building other probative evidence (use data, advertising reach, channel overlap).

Using market evidence to define the relevant public
- Be purposeful in framing the relevant public. If your evidence shows consumers cut across multiple categories (cardholders, deposit account holders, business banking customers), explain why the consumer group should be broader or narrower. Apex Bank highlights the problem when a party (or the Board) creates inconsistent definitions across factors.

Practical approach to assembling a strength record
1. Gather concrete, timestamped sales and advertising data tied to the mark.
2. Collect specimens that show use across channels and services.
3. If using a survey, hire a reputable survey firm experienced in trademark litigation; define the universe consistent with your DuPont two argument.
4. Combine quantitative evidence with narrative explanations that connect the evidence to the market definition applied under DuPont.

## Using third-party use to narrow an opponent’s scope of protection

Third‑party use evidence is the principal tool for arguing that a contested mark is weak and should receive only narrow protection. The TTAB and courts treat such evidence as probative of “marketplace crowdedness” and consumer sophistication: where many similar marks exist, consumers are better able to distinguish among them.

Types of third‑party evidence
- Third‑party registrations. Public records that a term is registered by multiple entities for similar goods/services can suggest the mark’s relative weakness. Registrations are easier to collect, but absent evidence of actual use they are less persuasive than third‑party use evidence.
- Third‑party use in commerce. Screenshots, press releases, Web Archives, app listings, and specimen evidence demonstrating real marketplace use are stronger. In financial services, evidence that many companies use a common term for cards, payments, and banking services undercuts a claim to broad exclusivity.
- Industry publications and trade press. Coverage showing multiple brands using the same core term in overlapping offerings supports the argument that consumers see the term as descriptive or commonly used.

Do not overreach: similarity of services matters
- Apex Bank’s reversal on DuPont six shows the limits: the relevant universe of third‑party marks for DuPont six must align with the finding on the similarity of services under DuPont two. If a party has established (or the Board finds) that services are highly similar—even legally identical—third‑party uses across the broader set are properly probative. But if the services are not shown to be similar, third‑party marks in unrelated fields should be discounted.
- Accordingly, structure your third‑party evidence so it matches the services comparison: if you argue your mark is weak because many financial services firms use the term across accounts, cards, and payments, show that those services are marketed to the same consumer cohort and through the same channels.

How to present third‑party evidence effectively
- Build a coherent narrative: show a market snapshot—who is using the term, in what services, and how consumers encounter those services.
- Use representative samples and explain selection: if you present 40 third‑party uses, explain why they are representative of a crowded field, not cherry‑picked anomalies.
- Distinguish registrations from uses: make clear which third‑party marks are merely registered and which are demonstrably used in commerce.
- Anticipate rebuttals: the opponent may argue many third‑party registrations are dead, abandoned, or narrow in geographic scope. Counter with dates, market reach, and proof of contemporaneous use where possible.

Strategic use: limiting an opponent’s scope
- If you are the applicant and face an opposer with an apparently strong mark, use third‑party evidence to demonstrate that the opposer’s mark lacks commercial strength and therefore should be entitled to only a narrow scope of protection. This can be a path to registration even when the dominant element of the mark is identical or similar.
- If you are the opposer, collect third‑party evidence that is inapplicable to your services to show why such evidence should be excluded; counter with your own examples of distinct, exclusive use.

## Mitigation strategies: co-existence, consent agreements, and limitations to identifications

Even in crowded fields, parties can often mitigate risk through negotiated solutions and careful application tactics. Apex Bank suggests that clarity of scope matters; agreements and amended identifications can create that clarity.

Coexistence and consent agreements
- Consent agreements are contractual settlements where one party agrees not to oppose a particular registration or use by the other, often with limitations (territory, channels, channels of trade, or a disclaimer). The TTAB treats written agreements as relevant to likelihood-of-confusion determinations and as persuasive evidence that confusion is unlikely where the parties have mutually agreed to coexist.
- For example, a bank that wants to use ASPIRE BANK while a payment processor uses ASPIRE on cards might agree to a consent: the processor will continue using ASPIRE on cards and the bank will use ASPIRE only for deposit accounts and will not venture into card issuance for a defined period. The parties can include quality control provisions and dispute resolution clauses.
- Recording the consent with the USPTO (via ESTTA) helps the registrant when defending against subsequent oppositions (the TTAB will consider the recorded agreement). Record the agreement contemporaneously and ensure it clearly defines the marks and the bounds of permitted use.

Limitations to identifications and amendments
- Exclusionary language can be negotiated up front or added by amendment during prosecution to avoid conflicts. Explicit exclusions—“excluding credit card issuance and processing services”—can prevent the TTAB from treating the application as covering those services.
- Narrowing an identification after registration is generally done through reissue or applying for a new registration with a narrower ID; the registrant must ensure ongoing use aligns with the narrower claim.
- Disclaimers can be used to relinquish rights in descriptive components; recorded disclaimers clarify that the registrant does not assert exclusivity over that term in isolation.

Other practical mitigation measures
- Channel and trade limitations: agree to restrict use to specified channels (e.g., card issuer uses in the business-to-business market, while the bank focuses on consumer retail channels). This can be particularly effective where the parties target different audiences.
- Territory limitations: geographically partitioning use can be effective in some cases (regional banks vs. national processors).
- Design differentiation: complement a shared word element with strong, distinctive logos or stylization so consumers perceive different sources even with similar word components.

Sample consent language (illustrative, non-exhaustive)
- “CC Serve consents to APEX’s registration of ASPIRE BANK for retail banking services excluding any services related to credit card issuance or card program management. CC Serve reserves the right to continue to use ASPIRE in connection with credit card servicing and payment processing services. The parties agree that such concurrent uses will not constitute a likelihood of confusion given the channel and consumer segmentation described herein.”
- Such language should be tailored to the facts and recorded with the USPTO.

Practical negotiation tips
- Start early. Contact potential opposers during clearance to test the potential for settlement. Many practical conflicts can be resolved without the time and expense of litigation.
- Be realistic. Dominant incumbents may demand significant concessions or license fees; weigh the licensing vs. litigation calculus.
- Use limited, time‑bound concessions. Where a mark owner is willing to tolerate new use but worried about dilution, consider phased expansions or territorial/time-limited coexistence to allow empirical market data to develop.

Conclusion: align identification choices, evidence gathering, and settlement tactics

Apex Bank’s central lesson is alignment: your identification of services, your choice of evidence for distinctiveness or weakness, and the universe of third‑party uses you rely upon must be coherent across all parts of your DuPont argument. Do not present a narrow service posture in one filing and then try to exclude relevant third‑party uses on another. Draft identifications deliberately; search widely; build a comprehensive record tailored to the relevant consumer group and channels; and consider practical settlements to avoid the expense and risk of opposition. When counsel designs a trademark program for a financial services client, success depends as much on a careful mapping of services and channels as it does on the creative strength of the mark.

# Significance and Takeaways from Apex Bank v. CC Serve

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve furnishes a focused, practice-oriented refinement of Section 2(d) likelihood-of-confusion analysis. The panel did not alter the DuPont framework, but it emphasized two interrelated rules of appellate review and adjudication that trial tribunals and practitioners must respect: (1) when a board (or court) makes a factual finding about the scope or similarity of goods or services under one DuPont factor, that factual scope should be carried consistently into the analysis of other factors that depend on the same baseline; and (2) the question whether a field is “crowded” under DuPont factor six admits no numerical bright line; adjudicators must weigh the quality, relevance, and breadth of third‑party evidence in context. The opinion therefore provides concrete guidance on how to present and rebut evidence about service overlap, third‑party uses, and mark strength in trademark oppositions and applications—particularly in the financial-services and fintech space where functional overlap and brand-sharing are commonplace.

Below I walk through the doctrinal teachings the opinion emphasizes, explain why they matter, and show how students and practitioners should apply them.

## Consistency rule clarified: align the scope of ‘similar goods/services’ across factors

Apex makes explicit what is implicit in the DuPont framework: findings about what goods or services are “similar” are factual predicates that should inform other related DuPont inquiries. In Apex the Board decided, after examining the identifications and definitions, that Apex’s “banking and financing services” and CC Serve’s “credit card services” were highly similar and in part legally identical. That finding drove the Board’s conclusion on DuPont factor two. But when the Board later considered DuPont factor six—the number and nature of similar marks in use—it narrowed the universe of third‑party marks it would count to those specified for “credit card services” only, excluding many third‑party marks that used “Aspire” in connection with broader banking or financing services. The Federal Circuit held that narrowing was inconsistent and thus erroneous: once the Board acceptably grounds a broad factual finding of similarity under factor two, it must retain that same scope when evaluating related factors that rest on service-similarity. The court vacated and remanded factors six and one for reconsideration consistent with factor two.

There are two practical legal implications. First, a tribunal’s factual resolution of the scope of services is not an isolated island. If the tribunal finds two services to be “similar” (or identical in part), that factual finding logically enlarges the universe of comparable third‑party marks and consumers considered under other factors. Second, parties should anticipate this cross-factor consistency and litigate the scope of services early and carefully: the baseline definition of what services are “similar” will affect which third‑party uses are relevant, which consumers are deemed the relevant public, and how probative survey or marketplace evidence will be treated.

How to apply the rule in practice. In litigation or opposition practice, if you win a finding that the applicant’s and registrant’s services overlap broadly, be sure to insist the tribunal use that same breadth when it assesses third‑party crowdedness (factor six), the relevant consumer class, and commercial impression (factor one). Conversely, if you are defending an applicant and you secure a narrow factual finding that the services differ, you should press for that narrow scope to limit the evidence the opponent may introduce under other factors. On appeal, a party should challenge any internal inconsistency where the fact‑finder’s own factual determination is then treated as narrower or different for subsidiary DuPont inquiries.

## No fixed numerosity for crowded-field showings; context and quality of evidence matter

Apex underscores that there is no magic number of third‑party marks that automatically establishes a field is “crowded.” The Federal Circuit has cited examples—fourteen marks in Jack Wolfskin or twenty‑six in Juice Generation—as contextually sufficient in those facts, but it refused in Apex to adopt a numerosity threshold that would exclude relevant uses solely because they fall outside a self‑imposed category (e.g., “credit card services” rather than broader “financial services”). The court directed the Board to reconsider factor six consistent with the broader similarity finding, implicitly instructing that the board must evaluate the whole body of third‑party evidence in the light of relevance, temporality, geographic scope, and the degree of similarity in services and channels.

What this means doctrinally is that DuPont factor six is qualitative as well as quantitative. A single, prominent third‑party use that consumers recognize widely may carry more weight than many obscure registrations. Similarly, contemporaneity (how recently the third parties used the mark), proof of actual commerce (evidence of use, not merely registration), and the closeness of the services offered to the parties’ services all affect probative value. Registrations alone are weaker than documented marketplace uses, and the evidentiary weight of third‑party registrations depends on their content (identifications, disclaimers, specimen dates).

How to marshal such evidence. For a plaintiff/registrant seeking to show a mark is strong (few similar third‑party marks), collect and emphasize high‑visibility, longstanding uses—national brands, consumer-facing materials, and evidence of consumer recognition. For a defendant/applicant seeking to demonstrate a crowded field, assemble multiple, contemporaneous third‑party uses across the broad class of similar services, including screenshots, press releases, and transactional materials showing the mark’s functional deployment (e.g., co‑branded cards, digital wallets, bank partnerships). Demonstrate overlap in channels and customers and show whether the third‑party uses are in fact consumer‑facing and likely to teach the public to distinguish the marks.

## Factor 2 can carry heavy weight where identifications are broad and overlap substantially

The Apex panel affirmed that the second DuPont factor—the similarity of the parties’ goods or services—can be dispositive when the identifications are broad and materially overlap. Courts and the TTAB frequently rely on the “identification of services” approach: where the parties’ identifications, considered at face value, encompass the same types of services, the services are treated as legally identical or highly similar without proof of actual marketplace overlap. In Apex, the Board’s analysis of dictionary definitions and the Trademark ID Manual led it to conclude that banking and financing include credit card functions, and that credit card services involve many of the same activities as banking (extending credit, servicing accounts, processing transactions). That was sufficient to support a finding that the services are highly similar and to give factor two substantial weight.

Students should understand two points about this dynamic. First, courts treat identifications with textual breadth as powerful evidence. A broadly worded identification of services can expose an applicant to a finding of legal identity even if the applicant in practice does not perform all listed activities (as Apex did not itself issue credit cards). Second, an opponent need not show marketplace competition or actual confusion if the statutory identifications demonstrate overlap—that is a common and permissible shortcut under DuPont when identifications are not limited.

Practitioner takeaways follow directly. Applicants who wish to avoid a strong factor‑two finding should craft narrower identifications; opposers should press for literal overlap between the identifications, and when overlap exists, argue factor two should be given major weight. Evidence useful to the opponent includes authoritative definitions, ID Manual entries (even deletions and historical entries, when relevant), and industry materials showing that services naturally subsume one another. Evidence useful to the applicant includes concrete evidence that the applicant’s services do not include the overlapping subset (e.g., affidavits, specimens, declarations, business plans showing no card issuance), and arguments showing different distribution channels, partners, and end‑users.

## Strength evidence reverberates: it recalibrates the similarity‑of‑marks analysis

Apex also reaffirms the doctrinal point that strength (or weakness) of a mark—the product of DuPont factor six—reverberates through other parts of the likelihood analysis, particularly the first DuPont factor (similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression). The Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s first‑factor analysis because it might be affected by a recalibrated finding on factor six: if the field is crowded and ASPIRE is conceptually or commercially weak, CC Serve’s mark is entitled to only a narrow scope of protection and the degree of similarity required to cause confusion is higher.

Conceptually, this follows the classic trademark principle: a strong, distinctive, and famous mark receives a broader scope of protection, and similar marks are more likely to create confusion; a weak or descriptive mark receives narrower protection, and similar marks may coexist without confusion. In the DuPont analysis, evidence of numerous third‑party uses is evidence of consumer sophistication in distinguishing brands and of limited distinctiveness of the contested mark; thus, the first‑factor calculus cannot be fully assessed in isolation from the sixth factor.

On the ground, that means practitioners must present a holistic record. An applicant facing a famous or conceptually strong mark should prepare to rebut strength evidence with proof of third‑party uses or argue differences in channels of trade and consumer sophistication. An opposer relying on a strong mark should develop evidence of market recognition (length of use, advertising expenditures, sales figures, media citations, consumer surveys) and be prepared to show that even in a crowded field their mark maintains acquired distinctiveness or consumer association.

## For practitioners: compile third‑party uses across the full breadth of similar financial services

Apex signals an actionable strategy for litigating and defending marks in the financial-services area: compile third‑party marks not only for the narrow literal label (e.g., “credit card services”) but across the entire spectrum of similar services that the fact‑finder has found to overlap (e.g., “banking,” “financing,” “payment processing,” “card program management,” “digital wallets,” and related fintech offerings). This is especially important where the services are functionally interconnected—credit card issuance, account servicing, transaction processing, and lending frequently interweave in the marketplace.

A thorough third‑party evidence program should include the following elements: (1) registrations and applications with their identifications and filing/use dates; (2) documentary evidence of actual use (screenshots, marketing pages, partner announcements, card images, account portals); (3) customer‑facing materials showing where consumers encounter the mark (apps, branches, card carriers); (4) evidence of partnerships (bank‑processor relationships, white‑label arrangements) that may indicate consumer perceptions of source; and (5) marketplace indicators of prominence (press coverage, awards, funding rounds in fintech, and market share). For each third party, counsel should explain why the use is relevant—tie it back to the DuPont finding on service similarity and to the channels and customer classes at issue. Dates matter: contemporaneous uses are more probative of the marketplace context at the time of the application or opposition.

Finally, custodial organization of evidence matters in practice. Produce annotated lists keyed to identifications, include geographic and temporal metadata, and prepare short legal memoranda explaining how each use affects the strength inference. The Apex opinion makes clear that tribunals will consider the quality and breadth of this evidence, not merely raw counts.

## For applicants: narrow or clarify identifications to shape channels and consumer assumptions

A key practical lesson from Apex is that an applicant’s choice of identification language on an intent‑to‑use application or registration can be dispositive. Broad, generic terms invite overlap findings; narrow, function‑specific identifications can constrain factor‑two overlap and thereby limit the relevant universe for factor six and the contours of the first‑factor commercial‑impression analysis.

When drafting or prosecuting marks in the financial sector, applicants should consider: (1) specifying the precise services offered (e.g., “retail branch banking services” or “online savings accounts” rather than “banking and financing services”); (2) excluding services the applicant does not provide (or disavowing credit card issuance or account servicing, if applicable); (3) identifying channels of trade and classes of consumers if they are distinct (e.g., “business banking for small enterprises”); and (4) including specimens or use-based evidence at filing that demonstrate actual and narrow commercial use. If the applicant genuinely does not provide a subset of services (as Apex did not issue credit cards), stating that clearly in prosecution can avoid an adverse factor‑two finding.

There are tradeoffs. A narrower identification may simplify registration and avoid oppositions, but it also narrows the protection the applicant obtains. Applicants must choose between a wider field of protection that risks opposition and a narrower grant that is more defensible. Where feasible, obtaining coexistence agreements or recorded consents from owners of similar marks may provide an alternative path.

## Anticipated applications in fintech and evolving financial products

Apex’s lessons are particularly salient in the evolving fintech landscape. Financial innovation has collapsed old functional boundaries—payment processors, card‑issuing banks, white‑label program managers, digital‑only banks, and app‑based wallets often perform overlapping functions and operate under many different brand relationships. This fusion makes factor‑two determinations frequently outcome-determinative and makes third‑party evidence more complex.

In fintech disputes, expect courts and the TTAB to evaluate not only the textual identifications but also the commercial realities of partnerships, APIs, co‑branding, and platform relationships. Parties should therefore assemble partnership agreements, service‑level descriptions, app screenshots, API documentation, and proof of end‑user touchpoints showing how the public encounters the service. Because many fintech services are digital and national from their inception, arguments about geographic separation will often fail; instead, the focus will be on channels of trade, consumer purpose, and the user experience.

Surveys and consumer perception evidence may be especially valuable in fintech cases to show whether consumers associate an Aspire‑type brand with a particular source or whether consumers are accustomed to multiple Aspire‑formative marks operating across payments, wallets, and banking. Apex suggests that such evidence, considered alongside a robust third‑party compilation, can affect both the perceived strength of a mark and the ultimate likelihood-of-confusion outcome.

## Conclusion: practical doctrine distilled

Apex Bank v. CC Serve does not rewrite DuPont; it enforces doctrinal coherence and evidentiary rigor. From a doctrinal perspective, the principal lessons are these: once the factfinder accepts a broad overlap of services under DuPont factor two, that breadth must govern the consideration of other factors that depend on service similarity (notably factors one and six); crowded‑field showings depend on the quality as well as quantity of third‑party evidence; and strength (or weakness) of a mark shifts the first‑factor analysis materially. From a practice perspective—especially in financial services and fintech—counsel must (1) define and litigate the factual scope of services early, (2) assemble high‑quality third‑party use evidence across the full span of similar services, (3) craft identifications carefully at prosecution to limit unintended overlap, and (4) think holistically about how strength evidence will change the calculus of mark similarity.

For students, Apex is an instructive case study in method: identify the baseline factual findings the tribunal must make, trace how those findings propagate through the DuPont factors, and scrutinize whether the tribunal’s treatment of evidence is internally consistent. The decision illustrates that the mechanics of evidence selection and the framing of identifications often determine the outcome as much as the literal similarity of words on a page.